/* Lessons for Google Cloud Certified Cloud Digital Leader: one per plan topic, matched by exact topic text.
   How to write and check them: docs/LESSON_GUIDE.md */
CertHub.addLessons("google-cdl", [
 {
  t: "What cloud computing is and why it drives digital transformation",
  body: [
   "Cloud computing is the on-demand delivery of computing resources, such as servers, storage, databases, networking, analytics and AI, over the internet, with pay-as-you-go pricing. Instead of buying and running hardware in your own building, you rent capacity from a provider such as Google Cloud and use it through a web console, a command-line tool or an API. The provider owns the data centers; you decide what to run and how much of it.",
   "The widely used definition from the US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) lists five characteristics. On-demand self-service means you can create resources yourself in minutes without asking anyone. Broad network access means you reach them over the network from many kinds of device. Resource pooling means the provider shares large pools of hardware across many customers. Rapid elasticity means capacity can grow and shrink quickly with demand. Measured service means usage is metered, so you pay for what you use.",
   "Digital transformation is a broader idea. It means an organization uses new digital technology to change how it works, how it serves customers and how it creates value. Moving servers to someone else's data center is not transformation by itself. Transformation is when a bank launches a new mobile feature in weeks instead of a year, a retailer personalizes offers from real-time data, or a hospital uses AI to reduce paperwork for nurses.",
   "The cloud drives this change because it removes many of the old limits. Teams no longer wait months for hardware, so they can experiment cheaply and drop ideas that fail. Managed services for data, AI and security give small teams capabilities that used to need large specialist groups. Global infrastructure lets a company reach customers in new countries without building data centers there. Google describes this combination of infrastructure, data, AI and collaboration tools as helping organizations become more agile and data-driven.",
   "For the Cloud Digital Leader exam, keep the business view in mind. Questions are usually framed around an organization's goal, such as speed, cost, innovation or reliability, and ask which cloud idea or product helps. Be ready to explain the difference between simply using cloud technology and actually transforming how the business operates."
  ],
  terms: [["Cloud computing", "On-demand access to shared computing resources over a network, paid for by use."], ["Digital transformation", "Using digital technology to change how an organization operates, serves customers and creates value."], ["On-demand self-service", "Users provision resources themselves, when needed, without human interaction with the provider."], ["Measured service", "Resource use is metered and reported, which enables pay-as-you-go billing."]],
  example: "A regional insurer used to wait three months for new servers before starting any project. After moving to Google Cloud it builds a claims-photo analysis prototype in two weeks using managed storage and a pre-trained image API, shows it to customers, and only then decides to invest further.",
  tip: "If an answer only changes where servers run but not how the business works, it is migration, not transformation. Look for answers about speed, experimentation, data-driven decisions and new customer value.",
  check: [["Name the five NIST characteristics of cloud computing.", "On-demand self-service, broad network access, resource pooling, rapid elasticity and measured service."], ["Why is moving servers to the cloud not the same as digital transformation?", "Transformation changes how the organization works and creates value; a move with no change to processes, culture or products only changes the location of the servers."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Business benefits of the cloud: scalability, elasticity, agility, reliability and strategic value",
  body: [
   "Organizations adopt the cloud for business reasons, and the exam expects you to match each benefit to a situation. Most questions describe a problem, such as a traffic spike, slow projects or outages, and ask which benefit or approach solves it.",
   "Scalability is the ability to handle more work by adding resources. You can scale up (vertically) by moving to a bigger machine, or scale out (horizontally) by adding more machines. Elasticity is scalability that happens automatically and in both directions: resources are added when demand rises and removed when it falls, so you do not pay for idle capacity. A shop with a holiday peak benefits from elasticity; a company growing steadily year after year benefits from scalability.",
   "Agility is the speed at which a business can try new ideas and respond to change. In the cloud a team can create an environment in minutes, test an idea and delete everything if it fails. That lowers the cost of failure and encourages experimentation. Managed services add to agility because teams spend less time on patching, backups and hardware, and more on features customers see.",
   "Reliability means a service keeps working when parts fail. Cloud providers offer many data centers grouped into zones and regions, and managed services that replicate data automatically. Designing across zones and regions lets an application survive failures that would take down a single on-premises server room. Flexibility is related: you can choose from many types of compute, storage and pricing to match each workload.",
   "Strategic value is the bigger picture. By moving undifferentiated work, such as running data centers, to a provider, a company can focus its people and money on what makes it different. It also gains access to capabilities it could not easily build, such as large-scale analytics, AI and global networks. Cost matters too, but the exam often treats cost savings as one benefit among several rather than the main reason."
  ],
  terms: [["Scalability", "The ability to increase capacity to handle more load, by scaling up or out."], ["Elasticity", "Automatically adding and removing resources as demand changes, so capacity follows load."], ["Agility", "The ability to move quickly: provision, experiment and change direction with little delay or cost."], ["Reliability", "The ability of a service to keep working correctly when components fail."]],
  example: "A tax-preparation website is quiet for ten months and extremely busy before the filing deadline. With autoscaling on Google Cloud it runs a few instances most of the year and hundreds in the final weeks, paying for the peak only while it lasts.",
  tip: "Scalability is about being able to grow; elasticity is growing and shrinking automatically with demand. A question that mentions short spikes followed by quiet periods is pointing at elasticity.",
  check: [["A startup wants to test a new product cheaply and drop it quickly if it fails. Which benefit is this?", "Agility: resources can be created and removed quickly with no up-front purchase."], ["What is the difference between scaling up and scaling out?", "Scaling up moves to a larger machine; scaling out adds more machines that share the load."]]
 },
 {
  t: "CapEx vs OpEx and total cost of ownership (TCO) when moving to the cloud",
  body: [
   "Capital expenditure (CapEx) is money spent up front on long-lived assets, such as servers, storage arrays, network gear and buildings. The asset is recorded on the balance sheet and depreciated over several years. Operating expenditure (OpEx) is ongoing spending on day-to-day costs, such as rent, electricity, salaries and subscriptions, recorded as an expense in the period it is used.",
   "Traditional IT is CapEx-heavy. A company forecasts what it will need for the next three to five years, buys it, and hopes the forecast was right. If demand is lower, hardware sits idle; if higher, customers wait while more is bought. The cloud turns most of this into OpEx: you pay monthly for what you use, you can stop paying when you stop using it, and fixed costs become variable costs that follow the business.",
   "Total cost of ownership (TCO) is the full cost of running something over its life, not just the purchase price. For an on-premises data center it includes hardware and its refresh cycle, software licenses, facilities and floor space, power and cooling, network connections, physical security, maintenance contracts, and the staff time to install, patch, monitor and repair everything. Many of these costs are hidden in other budgets, so comparisons that look only at server prices make on-premises look cheaper than it is.",
   "A fair cloud TCO also includes the costs of the move: migration work, training, running old and new systems in parallel for a time, and possible changes to licensing. On the cloud side, you should include ongoing usage, support plans and the time teams still spend on management. The benefit side includes things that are hard to price, such as faster delivery of new features.",
   "OpEx is not automatically cheaper. A cloud bill can grow quickly if resources are left running or sized too large, which is why cost management and FinOps practices matter. Predictable, steady workloads can use commitments to get lower prices, blending the flexibility of OpEx with some of the savings of planning ahead."
  ],
  terms: [["Capital expenditure (CapEx)", "Up-front spending on long-lived assets that are depreciated over time."], ["Operating expenditure (OpEx)", "Ongoing spending on services and running costs, expensed as it is used."], ["Total cost of ownership (TCO)", "All direct and indirect costs of a system over its life, including facilities, power, staff and refresh."], ["Depreciation", "Spreading the cost of an asset over its useful life for accounting."]],
  example: "A manufacturer compares the hardware price of a server refresh with a cloud estimate and at first finds the cloud more expensive. After adding its data center lease, electricity, cooling, maintenance contracts and two engineers' time, the on-premises TCO over five years turns out to be much higher than the cloud estimate.",
  tip: "The cloud moves spending from CapEx to OpEx, not the reverse. When a question mentions hidden costs such as power, cooling or staff, it is testing TCO.",
  check: [["Is a monthly cloud bill CapEx or OpEx?", "OpEx: it is an ongoing operating cost paid for what is used."], ["List three on-premises costs that are often missed in a TCO comparison.", "Any three of: power, cooling, floor space, hardware refresh, maintenance contracts, physical security, and staff time for operations."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Deployment options: on-premises, private cloud, public cloud, hybrid cloud and multicloud",
  body: [
   "Organizations can run IT in several places, and many use more than one. The exam expects you to recognize each model from a short description and to know why a company might choose it.",
   "On-premises means the organization owns or leases the facility and hardware and runs everything itself. It has full control but also full responsibility for capacity, maintenance and security. A private cloud offers cloud-style self-service and automation, but the infrastructure is dedicated to one organization, usually in its own data center. It gives control and isolation but still requires buying and running the hardware.",
   "A public cloud, such as Google Cloud, is run by a provider that shares large pools of infrastructure among many customers, who are isolated from one another logically. Customers get on-demand resources, global reach and managed services without owning hardware.",
   "Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private cloud infrastructure with a public cloud, connected so that workloads and data can work together. Common reasons are regulations that keep some data on-premises, recent investments in hardware that still has useful life, low-latency needs at a factory or store, or a gradual migration. A retailer might keep point-of-sale systems in stores and run analytics in Google Cloud.",
   "Multicloud means using more than one public cloud provider. Reasons include choosing the best service for each job, meeting regulator expectations about concentration risk, a merger with a company that uses another provider, or avoiding dependence on one vendor. An organization can be hybrid and multicloud at the same time. Google Cloud supports these strategies with open technologies such as Kubernetes and with GKE Enterprise, which manages clusters across environments, and with BigQuery Omni, which can analyze data stored in other clouds.",
   "Remember the key distinction: hybrid always involves private or on-premises infrastructure plus a public cloud; multicloud involves two or more public clouds."
  ],
  terms: [["On-premises", "Infrastructure the organization runs in its own or leased facilities."], ["Private cloud", "Cloud-style infrastructure dedicated to a single organization."], ["Hybrid cloud", "A combination of on-premises or private cloud with a public cloud, working together."], ["Multicloud", "Using services from two or more public cloud providers."]],
  example: "A hospital keeps its imaging archive in its own data center because of an existing contract, sends new scans to Google Cloud for AI-assisted analysis, and uses another provider's email service. It is running a hybrid and multicloud strategy at once.",
  tip: "Look for the words 'on-premises' or 'our own data center' in the scenario. If they appear alongside a public cloud, the answer is hybrid; if only several public clouds appear, it is multicloud.",
  check: [["A company uses Google Cloud and another public provider and has no data center. Which model?", "Multicloud, because it uses more than one public cloud and no private infrastructure."], ["Give two reasons a company might keep part of its IT on-premises in a hybrid design.", "Regulatory or contractual requirements, recent hardware investment, low latency at a local site, or a gradual migration."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Cloud service models: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS and serverless, and what the customer manages in each",
  body: [
   "Cloud services are grouped by how much of the stack the provider manages for you. Think of the stack from the bottom up: facilities, physical hardware, networking, virtualization, operating system, runtime and middleware, application, and finally data and access. The further up the provider manages, the less work you have, and the less control you have.",
   "Infrastructure as a service (IaaS) gives you virtual machines, disks and networks. The provider runs the physical layers and virtualization; you install and patch the operating system, runtimes and applications. Compute Engine is Google Cloud's IaaS offering. Choose IaaS when you need full control, such as for a legacy application that expects a particular operating system.",
   "Platform as a service (PaaS) gives you a managed platform where you bring your code and data. The provider runs the operating system and runtime, handles scaling and applies patches. App Engine is a classic Google Cloud PaaS. Developers get speed because they focus on code, not servers.",
   "Software as a service (SaaS) is a complete application used over the internet, such as Google Workspace (Gmail, Docs, Drive). The provider runs everything; you manage your users, settings and data.",
   "Serverless is a way of using cloud services where you do not provision or manage servers at all, capacity scales automatically (often to zero), and you pay for actual use such as requests or compute time. Cloud Run (containers), Cloud Run functions (event-driven code) and BigQuery (analytics) are serverless. Serverless services are often described as a form of PaaS, and some managed data services behave like SaaS for developers.",
   "Choosing a model is a trade-off between control and convenience. IaaS gives the most control and the most responsibility. SaaS gives the least of both. Many organizations mix them: SaaS for email, PaaS or serverless for new applications, and IaaS for workloads that cannot yet change."
  ],
  terms: [["IaaS", "Infrastructure as a service: virtual machines, storage and networks, with the customer managing the OS and above."], ["PaaS", "Platform as a service: a managed runtime where the customer brings code and data."], ["SaaS", "Software as a service: a complete application delivered over the internet."], ["Serverless", "Services with no servers to manage, automatic scaling and pay-per-use billing."]],
  example: "A company runs an old inventory system on Compute Engine because it needs a specific OS version, builds its new customer portal on Cloud Run, and gives staff Google Workspace for email. It uses IaaS, serverless and SaaS side by side.",
  tip: "Map products to models: Compute Engine is IaaS, App Engine is PaaS, Cloud Run and Cloud Run functions are serverless, Google Workspace is SaaS. The customer always manages its data and access, in every model.",
  check: [["Which service model gives the customer the most control over the operating system?", "IaaS, such as Compute Engine, where the customer installs and manages the OS."], ["What three traits define serverless?", "No servers to provision or manage, automatic scaling (often to zero), and paying only for actual use."]]
 },
 {
  t: "The shared responsibility model and how it changes with the service model",
  body: [
   "In the cloud, security and operations are shared between the provider and the customer. The shared responsibility model describes who does what. Misunderstanding it is a common cause of incidents: customers sometimes assume the provider protects things that are actually the customer's job.",
   "Google is always responsible for the security of the underlying infrastructure: the physical data centers, the hardware, the global network, and the virtualization layer that isolates customers. Google designs its own servers and network equipment and runs them with strict physical and operational controls.",
   "The customer is always responsible for its own data, for deciding who can access it (identities and permissions), and for how it configures the services it uses. If a customer makes a storage bucket public by mistake, or gives an employee far more access than needed, that is on the customer side of the line, even though the service itself is secure.",
   "Between those two fixed ends, the split moves with the service model. With IaaS, such as Compute Engine, the customer also manages the guest operating system, patches, installed software, and network firewall rules. With PaaS and serverless, such as App Engine or Cloud Run, Google also manages the operating system and runtime, so the customer focuses on application code, configuration and data. With SaaS, such as Google Workspace, Google runs the whole application, and the customer manages users, settings and data.",
   "Google Cloud describes its approach as shared fate: beyond drawing a line, it provides secure defaults, blueprints, guidance and tools to help customers do their part well. For the exam, remember two things. First, moving up from IaaS to SaaS shrinks the customer's responsibility but never removes it. Second, data, access and configuration always stay with the customer."
  ],
  terms: [["Shared responsibility model", "The division of security and operational duties between the cloud provider and the customer."], ["Shared fate", "Google Cloud's approach of actively helping customers secure their part through defaults, tools and guidance."], ["Security of the cloud", "The provider's duty: physical facilities, hardware, network and virtualization."], ["Security in the cloud", "The customer's duty: data, identities, access and configuration."]],
  example: "A company runs a web app on Compute Engine and does not patch the operating system for a year. An attacker exploits an old vulnerability. Because the OS is the customer's responsibility in IaaS, the fix is the company's job. Moving the app to Cloud Run would shift OS patching to Google.",
  tip: "Any answer that says the provider becomes responsible for customer data or access decisions is wrong. The question usually tests which layer moves to the provider as you go from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS.",
  check: [["Who patches the guest operating system on a Compute Engine VM?", "The customer, because Compute Engine is IaaS."], ["Which responsibilities stay with the customer even in SaaS?", "Its data, its users and their access, and the settings it chooses."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Google Cloud global infrastructure: regions, zones, edge points of presence and Google's private network",
  body: [
   "Google Cloud runs on the same global infrastructure that serves Google's own products. Understanding its building blocks helps you design for performance, reliability and data location, and it appears in many exam questions.",
   "A region is an independent geographic area, such as `us-central1` (Iowa) or `europe-west1` (Belgium). Each region contains several zones. A zone, such as `us-central1-a`, is an isolated deployment area within a region, with its own power, cooling and networking, so a failure in one zone should not affect the others. Zones in a region are connected by high-bandwidth, low-latency links. Some services are zonal (a VM lives in one zone), some are regional (replicated across zones in a region), and some are multi-regional or global.",
   "Designing across locations gives different levels of protection. Running in two or more zones protects against a zone failure. Running in two or more regions protects against a regional disaster and lets you serve users on different continents with lower latency. Region choice also matters for data residency rules, price (which varies by region) and carbon footprint.",
   "Google's network edge is made up of points of presence (PoPs), locations around the world where Google's network connects with internet service providers and other networks. User traffic enters Google's private network at a nearby PoP and travels most of the way on Google's own fiber, including subsea cables, rather than across the public internet. Services such as Cloud CDN cache content at the edge, closer to users.",
   "When you open the console you can see regions and zones in location drop-downs, and in Cloud Shell `gcloud compute regions list` lists them. The exact number of regions grows every year, so the exam focuses on concepts rather than counts."
  ],
  terms: [["Region", "An independent geographic area containing multiple zones, such as us-central1."], ["Zone", "An isolated deployment area within a region; a failure domain for zonal resources."], ["Point of presence (PoP)", "An edge location where Google's network connects to other networks near users."], ["Multi-region", "A large geographic area, such as US or EU, containing several regions, used by some storage and database services."]],
  example: "An online learning company serves students in Europe and Asia. It runs its app in europe-west1 and asia-southeast1, each across two zones, and uses Cloud CDN for video so content is cached at edge locations near students.",
  tip: "Zones protect against a data center failure; regions protect against a larger regional disaster and help with latency and data location. Edge PoPs are about getting traffic onto Google's network close to users, not about running your VMs.",
  check: [["An app must survive one zone failing. What is the minimum design?", "Run instances in at least two zones in the same region, for example with a regional managed instance group."], ["Name three things region choice affects.", "Latency to users, data residency, price and carbon footprint (any three)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Network performance basics: bandwidth, latency and choosing locations close to users",
  body: [
   "Two measurements describe most network performance questions. Bandwidth is how much data can be sent per second, like the width of a pipe. Latency is how long it takes a piece of data to travel from one point to another and back, like the length of the pipe. Throughput is the amount of data actually transferred in practice, which depends on both.",
   "Bandwidth matters for large transfers, such as uploading backups, streaming video or moving a data warehouse into the cloud. If a company has a slow internet link, even a small data migration can take weeks. Latency matters for interactive work: every click in a web app, every database query from a distant server, every video call. A page that makes twenty small requests to a server on another continent feels slow even on a fast connection, because each round trip adds delay.",
   "Distance is the main driver of latency, because signals in fiber can only travel so fast. That is why the location of your resources matters. Placing an application in a region near most users reduces latency. For global audiences, you can deploy in several regions and use a global load balancer to send users to the nearest healthy backend, and use Cloud CDN to cache static content such as images and videos at Google's edge locations.",
   "Google's private network also helps. Traffic that enters Google's network at a nearby point of presence travels over Google's backbone rather than hopping between many internet providers, which generally gives more consistent performance.",
   "Connecting an on-premises site to Google Cloud has options too. Cloud VPN sends encrypted traffic over the public internet and is quick to set up. Cloud Interconnect provides a private, dedicated or partner-provided connection with higher bandwidth and more predictable latency, suited to heavy hybrid workloads."
  ],
  terms: [["Bandwidth", "The maximum amount of data that can be carried per second on a connection."], ["Latency", "The delay for data to travel between two points, usually measured in milliseconds."], ["Cloud CDN", "Google Cloud's content delivery network, which caches content at edge locations near users."], ["Cloud Interconnect", "Private, high-bandwidth connectivity between an on-premises network and Google Cloud."]],
  example: "A game studio hosts its matchmaking servers in a US region. Players in Japan see delays even though their home connections are fast. The studio deploys servers in an Asian region and routes each player to the closest one, cutting round-trip time sharply.",
  tip: "Slow response with low server load usually points to latency from distance, fixed by choosing closer regions or a CDN. Slow bulk transfers point to limited bandwidth.",
  check: [["What is the difference between bandwidth and latency?", "Bandwidth is how much data can move per second; latency is how long each piece takes to arrive."], ["Name two ways to lower latency for users far from your region.", "Deploy in a region closer to them, and cache content at the edge with Cloud CDN (or use a global load balancer to reach the nearest backend)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Open source, open standards and avoiding vendor lock-in",
  body: [
   "Vendor lock-in happens when moving away from a provider becomes so costly or difficult that you are effectively stuck. It can come from proprietary data formats, APIs that exist only on one platform, skills that do not transfer, or contracts. Leaders worry about lock-in because it weakens their negotiating position and makes it harder to adopt better technology later.",
   "Open source software is software whose source code is publicly available to use, change and share under a license. Open standards are published specifications, such as SQL or HTTP, that many vendors implement. Building on them means your applications, data and skills can move between environments with less rework.",
   "Google has a long history of creating and contributing to open source projects that are now widely used. Kubernetes, the container orchestration system, began at Google and is now run by the Cloud Native Computing Foundation and available on every major cloud and on-premises. TensorFlow is an open source machine learning framework. Apache Beam, the programming model behind Dataflow, lets the same pipeline run on several engines. Google Cloud also offers managed versions of popular open source systems, such as Cloud SQL for MySQL and PostgreSQL, Dataproc for Apache Spark and Hadoop, and Memorystore for Redis.",
   "Openness is a deliberate part of Google Cloud's strategy. It supports hybrid and multicloud designs, lets customers bring existing tools and people, and lowers the risk of adopting the platform. Products such as GKE Enterprise build on Kubernetes so the same practices apply in Google Cloud, in your own data center and on other clouds.",
   "Open source is not the only way to reduce lock-in. Clear data export paths, standard file formats such as Parquet or CSV, and designs that separate business logic from provider-specific services also help. But the exam typically links 'avoid lock-in' and 'portability' with open source and Kubernetes."
  ],
  terms: [["Vendor lock-in", "Dependence on one provider that makes switching costly or difficult."], ["Open source", "Software whose code is openly available to use, modify and share under a license."], ["Open standard", "A publicly available specification that anyone can implement, such as SQL."], ["Portability", "The ability to move applications and data between environments with little change."]],
  example: "A media company worried about lock-in builds its services as containers on Kubernetes and stores analytics data in open formats. When a regulator later asks for an exit plan, the company can show that the same containers already run in a test cluster in its own data center.",
  tip: "When a question mentions portability, flexibility across environments or avoiding lock-in, look for open source, open standards or Kubernetes in the answers.",
  check: [["Which widely used open source project for containers started at Google?", "Kubernetes."], ["Name two Google Cloud services that are managed versions of open source software.", "For example Cloud SQL (MySQL, PostgreSQL), Dataproc (Spark, Hadoop), GKE (Kubernetes) or Memorystore (Redis)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Leading digital transformation: culture, skills and change management in a cloud adoption",
  body: [
   "Technology is usually the easy part of a cloud adoption. The harder part is people: how teams are organized, how decisions are made, which skills people have and how willing they are to work differently. The Cloud Digital Leader exam is aimed at people who help lead this change, so it expects you to understand the human side.",
   "Traditional IT often works in silos. Developers write code, a separate operations team runs it, security reviews happen at the end, and every change needs several approvals. The cloud makes it possible to work in smaller, cross-functional teams that own a product end to end, automate testing and releases, and ship small changes often. If the old processes stay, the new platform delivers little benefit.",
   "Culture matters as much as structure. Organizations that transform well encourage experimentation and treat failed experiments as learning, not blame. They make decisions with data rather than opinion, share information openly and give teams autonomy within clear guardrails. Google's own practices, such as blameless postmortems in Site Reliability Engineering, are examples of this culture.",
   "Skills are a common barrier. Teams need training in cloud concepts, security, data and new ways of working. Many organizations create a cloud center of excellence, a small group of experts who set standards, build shared foundations and help other teams adopt the cloud. Certifications and hands-on labs are part of building that capability across the business, not only in IT.",
   "Leadership support and change management tie it together. Leaders set a clear vision of why the change matters for customers, sponsor the effort, fund training, and measure outcomes such as release frequency, customer satisfaction or time to insight, not just servers migrated. Collaboration tools such as Google Workspace also support the change by making it easier for distributed teams to work together in real time."
  ],
  terms: [["Cloud center of excellence", "A cross-functional team that sets cloud standards, builds shared foundations and helps other teams adopt the cloud."], ["Silo", "A team or department that works in isolation from others, slowing hand-offs and decisions."], ["Change management", "A structured approach to helping people and organizations adopt new ways of working."], ["Blameless culture", "Reviewing failures to learn and improve systems rather than to punish individuals."]],
  example: "A bank moves several apps to Google Cloud but still needs six weeks of approvals for every release. After creating product teams with developers, operations and security together, automating deployments and forming a cloud center of excellence, it moves to weekly releases.",
  tip: "When the technology is in place but results are poor, the answer is usually about culture, skills, team structure or processes, not about buying more cloud resources.",
  check: [["What does a cloud center of excellence do?", "It sets standards and best practices, builds shared foundations such as the resource hierarchy and security guardrails, and helps other teams adopt the cloud."], ["Why does a blameless culture help transformation?", "People report and learn from failures openly, which encourages experimentation and improves systems instead of hiding problems."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Why data matters: using data to drive decisions, products and innovation",
  body: [
   "Every organization generates data: sales transactions, website clicks, sensor readings, support tickets, documents and more. On its own this data is just a cost to store. It becomes valuable when it is turned into insight that changes a decision, improves a product or creates something new. The Cloud Digital Leader exam treats data as a strategic asset and asks how Google Cloud helps unlock its value.",
   "Data creates value in a few broad ways. It improves decisions: a retailer that sees sales by store and hour can plan staffing and stock better than one relying on intuition. It improves customer experiences: recommendations, personalized offers and faster support all depend on data. It improves operations: predictive maintenance uses sensor data to fix machines before they break. And it enables new products and business models, such as selling insights or building AI features.",
   "Getting there is harder than it sounds. Data is often scattered across silos, in different formats, owned by different teams and of uneven quality. Older on-premises systems may not be able to store or process it at the scale needed, or may take hours to answer a question. Much of the most useful data is unstructured, such as images and text, which traditional tools handle poorly.",
   "The cloud helps by removing limits on storage and processing, and by offering managed services along the whole data journey. Data is ingested from many sources, stored in a suitable system, processed and cleaned, analyzed and visualized, and increasingly used to train and run machine learning models. Google Cloud offers services for each step, which later lessons cover: Cloud Storage and databases, Pub/Sub and Dataflow, BigQuery, Looker and Vertex AI.",
   "A data-driven culture matters too. Tools are only useful if people trust the data, can find it, and are encouraged to use it. That is why governance and accessibility are part of the exam's data section, not only technology."
  ],
  terms: [["Data-driven decision", "A choice based on analysis of data rather than intuition or habit alone."], ["Data silo", "Data held by one team or system that others cannot easily access or combine."], ["Data pipeline", "The automated flow that moves data from sources through processing to where it is used."], ["Insight", "An understanding gained from data that can guide an action."]],
  example: "A grocery chain combines till data, weather forecasts and local events in BigQuery. It learns that barbecue products sell three times faster on sunny weekends near parks, and it adjusts deliveries to those stores, cutting both waste and empty shelves.",
  tip: "Exam scenarios about 'unlocking value from data' usually point to breaking down silos, bringing data together in the cloud and making it accessible, not simply storing more of it.",
  check: [["Give three ways data creates business value.", "Better decisions, better customer experiences, more efficient operations, and new products or business models (any three)."], ["Why do data silos reduce value?", "Data that cannot be combined or accessed across teams cannot give a complete picture, so insights are missed or inconsistent."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Structured, semi-structured and unstructured data",
  body: [
   "Data comes in different shapes, and the shape decides where it is best stored and how it is analyzed. The exam expects you to recognize the three main types and match them to Google Cloud services.",
   "Structured data fits a fixed schema of rows and columns, where every record has the same fields with defined types. Customer tables, orders, bank transactions and inventory records are structured. It is easy to query with SQL and is typically stored in relational databases such as Cloud SQL, AlloyDB or Spanner, or in a data warehouse such as BigQuery for analysis.",
   "Semi-structured data has some organization, such as tags or keys, but not a rigid schema. Different records can have different fields, and fields can be nested. JSON documents, XML, application logs and many event messages are semi-structured. Document databases such as Firestore store it naturally, and BigQuery can store and query nested and repeated fields and JSON as well.",
   "Unstructured data has no predefined model: images, video, audio, emails, PDFs, scanned documents and free text. It makes up most of the data organizations create. It is usually stored as objects in Cloud Storage. To analyze it you often need AI: the Vision API can label images or read text in them, Speech-to-Text can transcribe calls, and the Natural Language API can find sentiment or entities in text. Once processed, the results become structured data you can query.",
   "The line between the types is not always sharp, and one project often uses all three. A customer support system might hold structured ticket records, semi-structured JSON event logs and unstructured call recordings. Choosing the right storage for each type, and connecting them for analysis, is a core part of data transformation on Google Cloud."
  ],
  terms: [["Structured data", "Data organized in a fixed schema of rows and columns, easily queried with SQL."], ["Semi-structured data", "Data with tags or keys but a flexible schema, such as JSON or XML."], ["Unstructured data", "Data with no predefined model, such as images, video, audio and free text."], ["Schema", "The definition of the fields, types and relationships in a data set."]],
  example: "An insurer stores policy records in Cloud SQL (structured), mobile app events as JSON in BigQuery (semi-structured) and photos of car damage in Cloud Storage (unstructured). It runs the photos through an image model and saves the results as structured fields for claims analysis.",
  tip: "Images, video, audio and documents are unstructured and usually go in Cloud Storage. JSON and logs are semi-structured. Tables with fixed columns are structured.",
  check: [["Is a JSON order record with optional fields structured, semi-structured or unstructured?", "Semi-structured: it has keys but not a rigid schema."], ["Where is unstructured data such as video usually stored in Google Cloud?", "In Cloud Storage, as objects in a bucket."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Databases, data warehouses and data lakes: what each is for",
  body: [
   "Three kinds of data store appear again and again in the exam. They are not rivals; most organizations use all three for different jobs.",
   "A database is built to support applications with many small, fast reads and writes of current data. This is called online transaction processing (OLTP): placing an order, updating an account balance, saving a user profile. Relational databases (Cloud SQL, AlloyDB, Spanner) use tables and SQL and enforce consistency with transactions. Non-relational or NoSQL databases (Firestore, Bigtable) use other models such as documents or wide columns for flexibility or massive scale.",
   "A data warehouse is built for analysis. It collects cleaned, structured data from many sources, often including historical data, and is optimized for complex queries that scan large amounts of data, known as online analytical processing (OLAP). Questions like 'What were sales by region and product for the last five years?' belong in a warehouse. BigQuery is Google Cloud's serverless data warehouse.",
   "A data lake stores large volumes of raw data in its original format, structured, semi-structured and unstructured, cheaply and at scale. Data is kept first and given structure later when it is used (schema on read). Data lakes support data science, machine learning and future uses that are not yet known. In Google Cloud, Cloud Storage is the usual foundation for a data lake.",
   "The approaches increasingly overlap. A lakehouse combines the low-cost, open storage of a lake with the governance and SQL performance of a warehouse. In Google Cloud, BigQuery can query data stored in Cloud Storage in open formats, and governance tools can manage both together.",
   "To choose, ask what the data is for. Running an application: database. Analyzing clean data for reports and dashboards: warehouse. Keeping large amounts of raw data of many types for later processing and ML: lake."
  ],
  terms: [["Database (OLTP)", "A store optimized for many small, fast transactions that run an application."], ["Data warehouse (OLAP)", "A store optimized for analytical queries over large amounts of structured, historical data."], ["Data lake", "A repository of raw data of any type, stored in its original format for later processing."], ["Lakehouse", "An approach combining data lake storage with warehouse-style management and SQL analytics."]],
  example: "An airline's booking app writes every reservation to a relational database. Each night the bookings are loaded into BigQuery, where analysts study routes and pricing. Raw flight-sensor files and maintenance photos are kept in a Cloud Storage data lake for engineers to use in machine learning projects.",
  tip: "If the scenario is about an application's day-to-day transactions, choose a database. If it is about analysis and reporting across sources, choose a warehouse (BigQuery). If it is about storing raw data of mixed types cheaply, choose a lake (Cloud Storage).",
  check: [["Which store is best for analyzing five years of sales from many systems?", "A data warehouse such as BigQuery."], ["What does 'schema on read' mean in a data lake?", "Data is stored raw, and structure is applied only when someone reads and processes it."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Cloud Storage and its storage classes: Standard, Nearline, Coldline and Archive",
  body: [
   "Cloud Storage is Google Cloud's object storage service. It stores any kind of file, called an object, in containers called buckets. It is designed for very high durability, scales without limits you need to plan for, and is used for website content, backups, data lakes, media, and data for analytics and machine learning. Objects are reached through the console, the `gcloud storage` command, client libraries or HTTPS.",
   "When you create a bucket you choose a location type. A regional bucket keeps data in one region, which suits data used by compute in that region. A dual-region or multi-region bucket stores data redundantly across a larger area, giving higher availability and geographic redundancy for content served widely.",
   "You also choose a default storage class, which trades storage price against access cost. Standard is for frequently accessed, or hot, data such as website images or data being actively processed. It has the highest storage price but no minimum storage duration and no retrieval fees. Nearline is for data accessed about once a month or less, with a 30-day minimum storage duration. Coldline is for data accessed about once a quarter, with a 90-day minimum. Archive is for data accessed less than once a year, such as long-term backups and regulatory records, with a 365-day minimum and the lowest storage price.",
   "Two points are often tested. First, all four classes give the same fast access to data when you read it; unlike some tape-style archives, Archive data is available in milliseconds, but reading it costs more. Second, colder classes charge retrieval fees and early-deletion charges if you delete data before the minimum duration, so putting frequently read data in a cold class can cost more, not less.",
   "Object Lifecycle Management rules can change an object's class or delete it automatically based on age or other conditions, for example move logs to Coldline after 90 days and delete them after five years. Autoclass can move objects between classes automatically based on how they are actually accessed."
  ],
  terms: [["Bucket", "A container for objects in Cloud Storage, with a name, location and default storage class."], ["Storage class", "A setting that trades storage price against access cost: Standard, Nearline, Coldline or Archive."], ["Minimum storage duration", "The period you are billed for even if you delete an object earlier: 30, 90 and 365 days for Nearline, Coldline and Archive."], ["Object Lifecycle Management", "Rules that automatically change the storage class of objects or delete them based on conditions such as age."]],
  example: "A law firm keeps active case files in Standard, moves closed cases to Coldline after six months with a lifecycle rule, and moves them to Archive after two years, where they stay for the legally required retention period at the lowest storage cost.",
  tip: "Match access frequency: daily use is Standard, monthly is Nearline, quarterly is Coldline, less than yearly is Archive. Remember that Archive still returns data in milliseconds; the trade-off is cost, not speed.",
  check: [["What is the minimum storage duration for Coldline?", "90 days."], ["Why might storing frequently read data in Archive cost more than Standard?", "Archive has retrieval fees on every read and a 365-day minimum, so frequent reads outweigh the lower storage price."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Relational databases on Google Cloud: Cloud SQL, AlloyDB and Spanner",
  body: [
   "Relational databases store data in tables of rows and columns with defined relationships, use SQL, and support transactions that keep data consistent: either all of a change happens or none of it does. They run most business applications, from online shops to banking. Google Cloud offers three main managed relational services, and the exam asks you to choose between them.",
   "Cloud SQL is a fully managed service for MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server. Google handles provisioning, patching, backups, replication and failover, so teams stop spending time on routine database administration. It is the natural target when you move an existing application's database to the cloud with few changes (a replatform). Cloud SQL scales well for most regional workloads, mainly by moving to larger machines (vertical scaling) and adding read replicas.",
   "AlloyDB for PostgreSQL is a fully managed, PostgreSQL-compatible database designed for demanding workloads. It targets high performance for transactional work and fast analytical queries on the same data. It suits organizations that want PostgreSQL compatibility but need more performance than a standard managed PostgreSQL instance provides.",
   "Spanner is a fully managed relational database that scales horizontally, adding capacity by adding nodes, while keeping strong consistency. It can span multiple regions and is built for very high availability. It supports SQL and transactions like a traditional database, but at a global scale that normally requires sharding by hand. Typical uses are global financial systems, inventory, gaming and any application that has outgrown a single database server.",
   "A simple way to choose: an existing MySQL, PostgreSQL or SQL Server application with normal scale, choose Cloud SQL. PostgreSQL applications that need higher performance or mixed transactional and analytical work, consider AlloyDB. Relational data that must scale horizontally or serve users worldwide with strong consistency, choose Spanner. Database Migration Service helps move existing databases into Cloud SQL and AlloyDB."
  ],
  terms: [["Relational database", "A database that stores data in related tables and uses SQL and transactions."], ["Cloud SQL", "Managed MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server databases."], ["AlloyDB", "A managed, high-performance PostgreSQL-compatible database."], ["Spanner", "A horizontally scalable, strongly consistent relational database that can span regions."]],
  example: "A regional online shop moves its MySQL database to Cloud SQL and stops worrying about backups and patching. Years later, after expanding to three continents, it moves its order system to Spanner so orders from every region write to one consistent database without manual sharding.",
  tip: "Keywords: 'existing MySQL/PostgreSQL/SQL Server, minimal changes' means Cloud SQL; 'global, horizontal scale, strong consistency' means Spanner; 'PostgreSQL-compatible, high performance' means AlloyDB.",
  check: [["Which relational service fits a globally distributed application that needs strong consistency?", "Spanner."], ["Which database engines does Cloud SQL support?", "MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Non-relational databases on Google Cloud: Firestore and Bigtable",
  body: [
   "Non-relational, or NoSQL, databases store data in models other than related tables. They trade some relational features, such as complex joins, for flexibility or very large scale. Google Cloud's two main managed NoSQL services on the exam are Firestore and Bigtable, and they solve quite different problems.",
   "Firestore is a serverless document database. Data is stored as documents, which are sets of fields similar to JSON, organized in collections. Documents in the same collection can have different fields, so the schema can evolve easily as an app changes. Firestore scales automatically with no servers to manage, and it has features built for web and mobile apps: real-time listeners that push changes to connected clients, offline support so an app keeps working without a connection and syncs later, and client libraries that work with Firebase. It is a good fit for user profiles, game state, chat, product catalogs and other app data.",
   "Bigtable is a wide-column NoSQL database built for very large amounts of data with consistently low latency and very high read and write throughput. Data is stored in tables with rows identified by a single row key, and columns grouped into families; rows can have many columns and empty cells cost nothing. It is ideal for time-series data, IoT sensor readings, financial market data, ad-tech and personalization, and it is the kind of system that powers large Google services. It is compatible with the open source Apache HBase API.",
   "The key to choosing is the workload. Firestore suits application data for web and mobile, where flexibility, real-time sync and zero administration matter and volumes are moderate to large. Bigtable suits huge analytical and operational data sets, often terabytes to petabytes, where throughput and latency at scale matter most and access is mainly by key.",
   "Neither is a data warehouse. For SQL analytics across large data sets you still use BigQuery, which can also query data stored in Bigtable."
  ],
  terms: [["NoSQL", "Databases that use models other than relational tables, such as documents, key-value or wide columns."], ["Firestore", "A serverless NoSQL document database with real-time updates and offline support for apps."], ["Bigtable", "A wide-column NoSQL database for very large, low-latency, high-throughput workloads."], ["Row key", "The single identifier used to store and retrieve rows in Bigtable, which determines how data is organized."]],
  example: "A fitness company stores each user's profile and workout plans in Firestore so the mobile app updates instantly across the user's phone and watch. Heart-rate readings from millions of wearables, arriving every second, go into Bigtable keyed by device and timestamp.",
  tip: "Mobile or web app, real-time sync, offline: Firestore. Massive time-series or IoT data with high throughput and low latency: Bigtable. Neither is the answer for SQL analytics, which is BigQuery.",
  check: [["Which service suits billions of sensor readings per day with low-latency reads by device?", "Bigtable."], ["Name two Firestore features aimed at mobile apps.", "Real-time listeners that push updates to clients, and offline support with later sync."]]
 },
 {
  t: "BigQuery: serverless data warehouse and analytics",
  body: [
   "BigQuery is Google Cloud's fully managed, serverless data warehouse. You load or stream data into it, or point it at data in other places, and query it with standard SQL. There are no servers, clusters or indexes to manage, and it can scan very large data sets, terabytes and beyond, quickly. It is one of the most important products for the data section of the exam.",
   "Its architecture separates storage from compute. Data is stored in a columnar format, which means a query reads only the columns it needs, and processing power is allocated on demand from a very large shared pool. Because the two are separate, you can store a lot of data cheaply and pay for processing only when you run queries. Pricing has two parts: storage, and compute for queries, either on-demand by bytes processed or through reserved capacity. That is why good practice is to select only the columns you need and to use partitioned tables, instead of writing `SELECT *` over everything.",
   "BigQuery does more than store tables. Data can arrive in batches or in real time through streaming. It can query data that stays in Cloud Storage or even in other clouds, through external tables. BigQuery ML lets analysts create and use machine learning models with SQL. Built-in geospatial functions, a large collection of public datasets, and connections to Looker, Looker Studio and spreadsheets make results easy to use.",
   "Access is controlled with IAM at the project, dataset, table and even row and column level, and data is encrypted at rest by default. Many data sources, including Google Ads and other SaaS tools, can be loaded on a schedule with the BigQuery Data Transfer Service.",
   "You can try it at no cost with the BigQuery sandbox, which offers a free monthly allowance of storage and query processing without a credit card. Querying a public dataset is a good first lab."
  ],
  terms: [["Data warehouse", "A central store of cleaned, structured data optimized for analytical queries."], ["Serverless", "No infrastructure to provision or manage; capacity is allocated automatically."], ["Columnar storage", "Storing data by column, so queries read only the columns they need."], ["BigQuery sandbox", "A no-cost way to try BigQuery within free monthly limits, without a billing account."]],
  example: "A streaming music service loads billions of play events into BigQuery each day. Analysts write SQL to find which songs are gaining listeners by country, marketing builds a Looker Studio dashboard on the results, and a data scientist trains a churn model with BigQuery ML in the same place.",
  tip: "For large-scale SQL analytics without managing infrastructure, the answer is BigQuery. Cloud SQL runs applications' transactions; BigQuery analyzes data across the business.",
  check: [["Why does BigQuery separate storage and compute?", "So each can scale independently: data is stored cheaply, and processing is allocated only when queries run."], ["What is one simple way to reduce BigQuery query cost?", "Select only the needed columns (avoid SELECT *) and filter on partitioned columns so fewer bytes are processed."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Streaming and processing data: Pub/Sub, Dataflow and Dataproc",
  body: [
   "Data rarely arrives ready to analyze. It must be collected from many sources, cleaned, joined and transformed, then delivered to where it is used. This can happen in batches, such as a nightly load of yesterday's sales, or as a stream, where events are processed continuously within seconds. Google Cloud has managed services for both.",
   "Pub/Sub is a global, asynchronous messaging service. Publishers, such as applications, devices or other services, send messages to a topic. Subscribers attached to that topic receive the messages. The publisher does not need to know who the subscribers are or wait for them, which decouples systems: if a downstream system is slow or offline, Pub/Sub holds the messages until it can process them. Pub/Sub is the usual entry point for streaming data, such as clickstreams, IoT telemetry and application events.",
   "Dataflow is a fully managed, serverless service for running data processing pipelines, both streaming and batch. Pipelines are written with Apache Beam, an open source programming model, and Dataflow handles the machines, scaling and fault tolerance. A common pattern is Pub/Sub to Dataflow to BigQuery: events are ingested by Pub/Sub, enriched and aggregated by Dataflow in real time, and written to BigQuery for dashboards and analysis. Google provides ready-made templates for common pipelines.",
   "Dataproc is a managed service for running Apache Spark, Hadoop and related open source tools. Clusters start in about a minute or two and can be deleted when a job finishes, so you pay only while they run. Dataproc is the natural choice when an organization already has Spark or Hadoop jobs and wants to move them to the cloud with little change.",
   "The choice: Pub/Sub moves messages and events; Dataflow transforms data in streaming or batch pipelines with no clusters to manage; Dataproc runs existing Spark and Hadoop workloads."
  ],
  terms: [["Pub/Sub", "A global messaging service where publishers send messages to topics and subscribers receive them asynchronously."], ["Dataflow", "A serverless service that runs Apache Beam pipelines for streaming and batch data processing."], ["Dataproc", "A managed service for Apache Spark, Hadoop and related open source tools."], ["Streaming data", "Data processed continuously as events arrive, rather than in scheduled batches."]],
  example: "A ride-sharing company publishes every trip event to Pub/Sub. A Dataflow pipeline calculates demand per neighborhood every ten seconds and writes the results to BigQuery, which feeds a live operations dashboard. Its older Spark fraud-scoring jobs run nightly on a Dataproc cluster that is deleted when they finish.",
  tip: "Ingesting events: Pub/Sub. Transforming data in real time without managing servers: Dataflow. Existing Spark or Hadoop code: Dataproc.",
  check: [["What does Pub/Sub decouple, and why is that useful?", "It decouples senders from receivers, so publishers do not wait for subscribers and messages are held if a subscriber is slow or offline."], ["A company has hundreds of existing Spark jobs. Which service moves them with the least change?", "Dataproc."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Business intelligence with Looker and Looker Studio",
  body: [
   "Business intelligence (BI) is the practice of turning data into reports, dashboards and answers that people across a business can use to make decisions. Data in a warehouse only creates value when someone can see and act on it, so BI is the final step of many data journeys. Google Cloud offers two related tools with different purposes.",
   "Looker is an enterprise BI and data platform. Its core idea is a semantic layer, written in a modeling language called LookML, where data teams define business entities and metrics once, for example what 'active customer' or 'net revenue' means and how it is calculated. Everyone who explores data, builds a dashboard or asks a question then uses those same definitions, so different departments get consistent numbers. Looker queries the database directly, such as BigQuery, instead of copying data into its own store, so results are current and access rules are enforced. It also supports scheduled reports, alerts and embedding analytics in other applications.",
   "Looker Studio (formerly Data Studio) is a free, self-service tool for building interactive dashboards and reports. It connects to many sources, including BigQuery, Google Sheets, Google Analytics and Google Ads, and is easy for business users to start with. Reports can be shared like Google Docs. A paid Looker Studio Pro edition adds enterprise management features.",
   "The choice depends on scale and governance needs. A marketing analyst who needs a quick dashboard from a spreadsheet can use Looker Studio in minutes. A large company that needs trusted, consistent metrics across hundreds of users and teams, with governed access, will use Looker.",
   "BI connects with the rest of the platform: data arrives through Pub/Sub, Dataflow or transfer services, is stored in BigQuery, and is presented in Looker or Looker Studio. Increasingly, AI assistants let users ask questions in natural language, but they rely on the same well-defined data underneath."
  ],
  terms: [["Business intelligence (BI)", "Tools and practices that turn data into reports and dashboards for decision making."], ["Semantic layer", "A shared definition of business metrics and data relationships used by all reports."], ["LookML", "Looker's modeling language for defining dimensions, measures and relationships."], ["Looker Studio", "Google's free, self-service dashboard and reporting tool."]],
  example: "Sales and finance at a software company report different 'annual recurring revenue' numbers. The data team defines the metric once in LookML on top of BigQuery, and both teams' Looker dashboards now show the same figure.",
  tip: "Consistent, governed metrics across the enterprise point to Looker and its semantic model. A quick, free dashboard from many sources points to Looker Studio.",
  check: [["What problem does Looker's semantic layer solve?", "Inconsistent metrics: definitions are written once in LookML and reused everywhere, so reports agree."], ["Name two data sources Looker Studio can connect to.", "For example BigQuery, Google Sheets, Google Analytics or Google Ads."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Moving data to Google Cloud: Database Migration Service, BigQuery Data Transfer Service, Storage Transfer Service and Transfer Appliance",
  body: [
   "Before data can create value in the cloud, it has to get there. Google Cloud offers several transfer services, and the exam tests whether you can pick the right one from the type of data, its source and the network available.",
   "Database Migration Service moves databases, such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server and Oracle, into Cloud SQL or AlloyDB. It can perform continuous replication so the source stays in use during migration, which keeps downtime short: you let the target catch up, then switch the application over. It is serverless and manages the migration process for you.",
   "BigQuery Data Transfer Service automates loading data into BigQuery on a schedule. It has connectors for Google sources such as Google Ads, YouTube and Google Play, for many third-party SaaS applications, for files in Cloud Storage and other clouds' object stores, and for migrating from other data warehouses. Marketing and analytics teams use it to keep BigQuery up to date without writing pipelines.",
   "Storage Transfer Service moves large amounts of file or object data over the network into Cloud Storage, from other cloud providers' storage, from HTTP locations, or from on-premises file systems through agents. It supports scheduled and repeated transfers, so it can also keep buckets in sync.",
   "Transfer Appliance is a physical, high-capacity storage device that Google ships to your data center. You copy data onto it, ship it back, and Google uploads the data to Cloud Storage. Data on the appliance is encrypted. It is the right choice when the amount of data is so large, or the network so slow, that an online transfer would take too long, for example hundreds of terabytes over a modest connection.",
   "For the exam: databases to Cloud SQL or AlloyDB, Database Migration Service. Scheduled loads into BigQuery from SaaS and other sources, BigQuery Data Transfer Service. Files and objects over the network into Cloud Storage, Storage Transfer Service. Huge data sets with limited bandwidth, Transfer Appliance."
  ],
  terms: [["Database Migration Service", "A managed service that migrates databases into Cloud SQL or AlloyDB with minimal downtime."], ["BigQuery Data Transfer Service", "Scheduled, managed data loads into BigQuery from Google, SaaS and other sources."], ["Storage Transfer Service", "Online transfers of object and file data into Cloud Storage from other clouds, HTTP or on-premises."], ["Transfer Appliance", "A physical device shipped to a customer to move very large data sets offline into Google Cloud."]],
  example: "A media company has 600 TB of video on-premises with a slow internet link, so it uses Transfer Appliance. Its PostgreSQL database moves to Cloud SQL with Database Migration Service while staying online, and its ad performance data now lands in BigQuery every morning via BigQuery Data Transfer Service.",
  tip: "Watch for clues about network speed and data size. 'Slow connection' and 'hundreds of terabytes' mean Transfer Appliance. 'Minimal downtime database move' means Database Migration Service.",
  check: [["Which service keeps a source database in use while it is copied to Cloud SQL?", "Database Migration Service, using continuous replication before cutover."], ["A company wants data from another cloud's object storage copied to Cloud Storage every night. Which service?", "Storage Transfer Service."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Data governance: quality, security, access control and cataloging",
  body: [
   "Data governance is the set of policies, roles and tools that make sure data is accurate, secure, discoverable and used appropriately. Without it, organizations collect more data but trust it less: nobody knows which table is correct, sensitive data leaks to the wrong people, and regulators ask questions nobody can answer. Good governance is what makes data safe and useful at the same time.",
   "Governance has several parts. Data quality means data is accurate, complete, consistent and up to date; poor quality leads to wrong decisions and poor ML models. Ownership and stewardship mean each data set has someone accountable for it. Security and privacy mean sensitive data such as personal or financial information is protected and handled according to law and policy. Lifecycle management decides how long data is kept and when it is deleted. Lineage records where data came from and how it was transformed, which helps with trust and audits.",
   "Access control is central. In Google Cloud, IAM decides who can view or change data at the project, dataset, table or bucket level, and BigQuery adds column-level and row-level security so, for example, only HR can see salary columns. Following least privilege limits damage from mistakes or compromised accounts. Sensitive Data Protection can discover and classify sensitive data such as credit card numbers and can mask or de-identify it.",
   "Cataloging makes data discoverable. A data catalog records what data exists, where it lives, what it means, who owns it and how sensitive it is, so analysts can find trusted data instead of creating copies. In Google Cloud, Dataplex provides data cataloging, quality checks, lineage and governance across data lakes and warehouses.",
   "Governance should enable, not only restrict. The goal is that the right people can quickly find and use trustworthy data, while sensitive data stays protected."
  ],
  terms: [["Data governance", "Policies, roles and tools that keep data accurate, secure, discoverable and properly used."], ["Data lineage", "A record of where data came from and how it was transformed."], ["Data catalog", "An inventory of data assets with descriptions, owners and classifications so people can find them."], ["Column-level security", "Restricting access to specific columns, such as sensitive fields, in a table."]],
  example: "A healthcare provider catalogs all its datasets in Dataplex, tags patient identifiers as sensitive, restricts those columns in BigQuery to a small clinical group, and runs automatic quality checks. Analysts can now find approved datasets in minutes instead of asking around.",
  tip: "Questions about finding trusted data, knowing who owns it, tracking where it came from and limiting who sees sensitive fields are all about data governance.",
  check: [["Name four parts of data governance.", "Any four of: data quality, ownership, security and privacy, access control, lifecycle and retention, lineage, cataloging."], ["How can BigQuery let most analysts query a table but hide one sensitive column?", "Use column-level security (policy tags) so only authorized users can read that column."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Artificial intelligence, machine learning and generative AI: definitions and differences",
  body: [
   "These three terms are often used interchangeably, but they are nested ideas, and the exam expects you to tell them apart.",
   "Artificial intelligence (AI) is the broad field of building computer systems that perform tasks normally requiring human intelligence, such as understanding language, recognizing images, making decisions or solving problems. Early AI often relied on hand-written rules: if the customer says 'refund', route to billing. Rules work when the logic is clear, but they break down when the problem has too many cases to write down.",
   "Machine learning (ML) is a subset of AI in which systems learn patterns from data instead of being programmed with explicit rules. You give a model many examples, such as thousands of emails labeled spam or not spam, and a training process adjusts the model until it can make accurate predictions on new examples it has never seen. The main kinds include supervised learning (learning from labeled examples to predict a label or number), unsupervised learning (finding structure such as clusters in unlabeled data) and reinforcement learning (learning by trial and reward). Common business uses are classification, forecasting, recommendations and anomaly detection.",
   "Deep learning is a type of ML that uses neural networks with many layers. It is especially good at unstructured data such as images, audio and text, and it powers most modern AI.",
   "Generative AI is a type of deep learning that creates new content, such as text, images, code, audio or video, rather than only predicting a label. It is built on foundation models: very large models trained on broad data that can be adapted to many tasks. Large language models (LLMs), such as Google's Gemini models, are foundation models for language. You interact with them through a prompt, an instruction or question written in natural language.",
   "In short: AI is the goal, ML is the most common way to reach it, deep learning is a powerful kind of ML, and generative AI is deep learning that produces new content."
  ],
  terms: [["Artificial intelligence (AI)", "The field of building systems that perform tasks that normally need human intelligence."], ["Machine learning (ML)", "A subset of AI where systems learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules."], ["Generative AI", "AI that creates new content, such as text, images or code, from a prompt."], ["Foundation model", "A large model trained on broad data that can be adapted to many tasks; LLMs are one kind."]],
  example: "An online retailer uses a rules engine to apply shipping prices (not ML), an ML model to predict which customers may cancel their subscription (predictive ML), and a generative AI model to draft personalized product descriptions (generative AI).",
  tip: "If the system produces new content from a prompt, it is generative AI. If it predicts a category or number from past examples, it is traditional (predictive) ML. If it follows fixed if-then logic, it is not ML at all.",
  check: [["How does machine learning differ from traditional programming?", "Traditional programs follow rules written by people; ML models learn the rules or patterns from example data."], ["What is a foundation model?", "A large model trained on broad data that can be adapted to many downstream tasks, such as an LLM."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Business problems machine learning can solve, and when ML is not the right tool",
  body: [
   "Machine learning is powerful, but it is a tool for specific kinds of problems. A digital leader should be able to spot where ML adds value and where simpler approaches are better.",
   "ML works well when there is a pattern in data that is too complex to write as rules, and plenty of examples of that pattern. Classic business uses include predicting outcomes (which customers are likely to churn, how much stock a store will need next week), classifying items (spam or not, which department should handle a ticket, whether a transaction looks fraudulent), recommending products or content, detecting anomalies (unusual sensor readings or payments), and understanding unstructured data (reading documents, recognizing objects in images, transcribing calls). Generative AI adds summarizing, drafting, translating, answering questions and writing code.",
   "ML is also valuable where scale or speed exceed what people can do. A person can review a few hundred insurance claims a day; a model can score millions and flag the unusual ones for human review. That frees people for judgment and customer contact.",
   "ML is not the right tool when the rules are known and exact, such as calculating tax, interest or shipping by a published formula; normal code is cheaper, faster and always correct. It is a poor fit when there is little or no relevant data, when the data does not reflect the situation the model will face, or when every decision must be fully explainable and a model cannot provide that. It is also unnecessary when a simple report or dashboard would answer the question.",
   "Before starting an ML project, define the business problem and how success will be measured, confirm that suitable data exists, and consider whether a pre-trained service already solves it. ML projects are experiments: plan for iteration, monitoring and retraining, because the world changes and model accuracy can drift over time."
  ],
  terms: [["Classification", "Predicting which category an item belongs to, such as fraud or not fraud."], ["Forecasting", "Predicting future numeric values, such as demand next month."], ["Anomaly detection", "Finding data points that differ significantly from normal patterns."], ["Model drift", "A drop in model accuracy over time as real-world data changes from the training data."]],
  example: "A telecom company wants to predict which customers will cancel. It has five years of usage and cancellation history, so it builds a churn model and gives retention offers to high-risk customers. It does not use ML for calculating monthly bills, which follow fixed price rules.",
  tip: "Exam distractors often suggest ML for tasks with exact rules. Choose ML when patterns are complex and data is plentiful; choose ordinary code when the logic is known.",
  check: [["Give three business problems suited to ML.", "Any three: churn prediction, demand forecasting, fraud detection, recommendations, document or image understanding, anomaly detection."], ["Why is ML a poor choice for calculating sales tax?", "The rules are exact and known, so normal code gives correct results; a model could only approximate them."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Data quality for machine learning: accuracy, completeness, representativeness and bias",
  body: [
   "A machine learning model learns only from the data it is trained on. If that data is poor, the model will be poor, however advanced the algorithm. This is often summarized as 'garbage in, garbage out', and it is why data quality is part of the AI section of the exam.",
   "Several dimensions of quality matter. Accuracy: values must be correct. Mislabeled training examples, such as fraudulent transactions marked as normal, teach the model the wrong thing. Completeness: missing values or missing records leave gaps in what the model can learn. Consistency: the same thing should be recorded the same way across sources, such as dates and units. Timeliness: data should reflect current conditions; a demand model trained only on pre-pandemic data may fail today. Relevance: the features used should actually relate to what you want to predict.",
   "Representativeness is especially important. Training data must cover the full range of cases the model will see in real use. A model trained mostly on customers from one city, one age group or one type of device may perform badly or unfairly for others. That leads to bias: systematic errors that disadvantage certain groups. Bias can come from historical data that reflects past unfair decisions, from how data was collected, or from how it was labeled.",
   "Volume matters too. More relevant, good-quality examples generally help a model learn, but a huge amount of biased or wrong data does not.",
   "Improving quality is ongoing work. Teams profile data to find missing or odd values, clean and standardize it, check that important groups are represented, have labels reviewed, and monitor model performance across groups after deployment. Governance tools that track data lineage and quality help. In Google Cloud, data can be prepared in BigQuery or Dataflow, and Vertex AI offers tools to evaluate models and explain their predictions."
  ],
  terms: [["Training data", "The examples a model learns from."], ["Representative data", "Data that covers the full range of cases and groups the model will face in real use."], ["Bias (in ML)", "Systematic errors in a model's output that unfairly favor or disadvantage certain groups."], ["Label", "The correct answer attached to a training example in supervised learning."]],
  example: "A bank's credit model trained on ten years of approvals gives lower scores to applicants from certain neighborhoods, because past human decisions in the data were biased. The team rebalances the data, removes features that act as proxies for protected characteristics and checks accuracy for each group before release.",
  tip: "When a model works well for one group but poorly for another, the most likely cause is unrepresentative training data, not a lack of computing power.",
  check: [["List four dimensions of data quality for ML.", "Accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, relevance and representativeness (any four)."], ["How can historical data introduce bias?", "If past decisions in the data were unfair, the model learns and repeats those patterns."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Responsible AI: Google's AI Principles, fairness, explainability, privacy and accountability",
  body: [
   "AI systems can make decisions that affect people's jobs, loans, health and safety, and generative AI can produce content that is wrong or harmful. Responsible AI is the practice of designing, building and using AI in ways that are safe, fair and trustworthy. Google Cloud treats it as part of every AI project, and the exam expects you to know its main ideas.",
   "Google published its AI Principles in 2018 to guide its own work, and has updated them since. They commit Google to developing AI that is socially beneficial, avoids creating or reinforcing unfair bias, is built and tested for safety, is accountable to people, incorporates privacy by design, and upholds high standards of scientific excellence, while avoiding applications likely to cause overall harm. The exact wording has evolved, so focus on the themes rather than memorizing a list.",
   "Fairness means the system does not produce unjustly different outcomes for different groups. Teams check training data for representativeness and measure model performance across groups. Explainability means people can understand why a model made a prediction, such as which factors increased a risk score. It supports trust, debugging and regulatory reviews; Vertex AI provides explanation features for many models. Privacy means personal data is collected and used only as appropriate, protected, and minimized; techniques include de-identification and strict access control. Accountability means humans remain responsible: there are owners, reviews, documentation and ways for affected people to question decisions. Safety and security mean testing for failure modes, misuse and attacks, and for generative AI, filtering harmful output.",
   "Responsible AI is not a one-time check. It runs through the lifecycle: choosing the problem, collecting data, training, evaluating, deploying and monitoring. Keeping a human in the loop for high-impact decisions is a common safeguard.",
   "For customers, Google Cloud also states that it does not use customer data from its enterprise AI services to train its own models without permission, which addresses a common concern about sharing data with AI tools."
  ],
  terms: [["Responsible AI", "Designing and using AI so that it is fair, safe, private, explainable and accountable."], ["Fairness", "Avoiding unjust differences in outcomes for different groups of people."], ["Explainability", "The ability to describe why a model produced a given output."], ["Human in the loop", "A design where a person reviews or approves AI outputs before important actions."]],
  example: "A recruiting firm uses AI to shortlist candidates. It tests the model's results across genders and ethnic groups, shows recruiters which skills drove each recommendation, keeps a recruiter responsible for every final decision, and lets candidates request a human review.",
  tip: "Match the scenario to the principle: 'why did the model decide this' is explainability, 'different results for different groups' is fairness, 'who is responsible' is accountability, 'personal data' is privacy.",
  check: [["What is explainability and why does it matter?", "Being able to describe the factors behind a prediction; it builds trust, helps find errors and supports audits and regulation."], ["Give one safeguard for high-impact AI decisions.", "Keep a human in the loop to review or approve decisions, and monitor outcomes across groups."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Pre-trained AI APIs: Vision, Natural Language, Speech-to-Text, Text-to-Speech and Translation",
  body: [
   "The fastest way to add AI to an application is to call a pre-trained model through an API. Google has already trained these models on very large data sets, so you send your data, such as an image, a document or an audio file, and receive results in seconds. You need no training data, no ML expertise and no infrastructure, and you typically pay per use, with a free monthly allowance for many of them.",
   "The Cloud Vision API analyzes images. It can detect objects and labels (such as 'dog' or 'beach'), read printed and handwritten text through optical character recognition (OCR), detect faces (not identify people), landmarks and logos, and flag explicit content with SafeSearch. The Video Intelligence API does similar analysis on video.",
   "The Cloud Natural Language API analyzes text. It can find sentiment (positive or negative), extract entities such as people, places and products, classify content into categories and analyze syntax. It is useful for reviewing customer feedback, routing support tickets or organizing documents.",
   "Speech-to-Text converts spoken audio into text, which is useful for transcribing calls, adding captions and voice commands. Text-to-Speech does the opposite, producing natural-sounding speech from text in many languages and voices, for example for phone systems or accessibility. The Cloud Translation API translates text between many languages and can detect the source language.",
   "Document AI is a related service for extracting structured data, such as fields and tables, from documents like invoices, receipts and forms.",
   "Pre-trained APIs are ideal when your problem is a common one that general models already handle well. If you need to recognize things specific to your business, such as your own product defects or industry jargon, you may need a customized model through AutoML or custom training instead. On the exam, choose a pre-trained API when a scenario stresses speed, no ML team and a common task."
  ],
  terms: [["Pre-trained model", "A model already trained by the provider that you can use immediately without your own training data."], ["OCR", "Optical character recognition: extracting text from images of printed or handwritten documents."], ["Sentiment analysis", "Determining whether text expresses a positive, negative or neutral opinion."], ["Entity extraction", "Identifying names of people, places, organizations and other items in text."]],
  example: "A hotel chain sends every guest review to the Natural Language API to score sentiment and pull out mentioned topics such as 'breakfast' or 'wifi'. Negative reviews about specific topics are routed to the right manager the same day, with no data science team involved.",
  tip: "Common task plus no ML skills plus need it quickly equals a pre-trained API. The Vision API reads text in images; the Natural Language API analyzes meaning in text; Translation converts between languages.",
  check: [["Which API would you use to transcribe recorded customer calls?", "Speech-to-Text."], ["When is a pre-trained API not enough?", "When the task is specific to your business, such as your own product defects or specialized terms, so a customized model is needed."]]
 },
 {
  t: "BigQuery ML: building and using models with SQL where the data already lives",
  body: [
   "Many organizations keep their most valuable business data in BigQuery, and many of their analysts know SQL well but not Python or ML frameworks. BigQuery ML closes that gap: it lets you create, train, evaluate and use machine learning models with SQL statements, directly inside BigQuery.",
   "The workflow looks like normal SQL. You write a `CREATE MODEL` statement that names the model type and selects the training data from a table, and BigQuery trains it. `ML.EVALUATE` reports how accurate the model is, and `ML.PREDICT` applies it to new rows, returning predictions as query results you can join, store or show in a dashboard. For example, a model could predict whether each customer will buy again, based on their past orders.",
   "BigQuery ML supports common model types, including linear regression for predicting numbers, logistic regression for yes-or-no classification, clustering to group similar customers, time-series forecasting for demand, and recommendation models. It can also connect to models hosted in Vertex AI, including generative AI models, so analysts can call them from SQL, for example to summarize text columns.",
   "The key advantages are speed and simplicity. Data does not have to be exported to a separate ML system, which saves time, reduces copies of sensitive data and keeps existing access controls. Analysts can build useful models without waiting for scarce data scientists. Training uses BigQuery's scalable processing, and you pay through normal BigQuery pricing.",
   "BigQuery ML is not meant for every problem. Highly customized deep learning, unusual model architectures or complex unstructured data are better handled with Vertex AI custom training. But for many structured-data business predictions, it is the quickest path from data to results. You can try it in the BigQuery sandbox with a small public dataset."
  ],
  terms: [["BigQuery ML", "A BigQuery feature for creating, training and using ML models with SQL."], ["CREATE MODEL", "The SQL statement that defines and trains a model in BigQuery ML."], ["ML.PREDICT", "The BigQuery ML function that applies a trained model to data to produce predictions."], ["Logistic regression", "A model type that predicts the probability of a yes-or-no outcome."]],
  example: "A subscription box company's analysts write a `CREATE MODEL` query on two years of customer data in BigQuery to predict who is likely to cancel. They run `ML.PREDICT` each week and feed the results into a Looker Studio dashboard for the retention team, all without moving any data.",
  tip: "SQL-skilled analysts plus data already in BigQuery plus a common prediction task equals BigQuery ML. It avoids moving data and needs no Python.",
  check: [["What are two benefits of BigQuery ML?", "Analysts can use SQL instead of learning new tools, and data stays in BigQuery with no export, keeping it secure and saving time."], ["Which BigQuery ML function returns predictions?", "ML.PREDICT."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Vertex AI: the unified ML platform, AutoML and custom training",
  body: [
   "Vertex AI is Google Cloud's unified platform for building, deploying and managing machine learning and generative AI. Before unified platforms, teams stitched together separate tools for preparing data, training, tracking experiments, serving models and monitoring them. Vertex AI brings these steps together, so data scientists and engineers work in one place with consistent security and governance.",
   "Vertex AI offers two main ways to build your own predictive models. AutoML lets you train a high-quality model on your own labeled data with little or no code. You provide the data, for example images labeled 'defect' or 'no defect', or a table with a target column, choose the objective, and AutoML handles model selection and tuning. It suits teams with domain data but limited ML expertise, and it supports tabular, image, text and video data.",
   "Custom training gives full control. Data scientists write their own training code using frameworks such as TensorFlow, PyTorch or scikit-learn, choose the machine types, GPUs or TPUs, and tune every detail. It is the right choice when the problem is unique, when the model is a source of competitive advantage, or when AutoML cannot meet the requirements. It needs more skill and time.",
   "Around training, Vertex AI provides the tools needed to run ML in production, often called MLOps: managed datasets and labeling, Workbench notebooks, pipelines to automate workflows, a model registry to track versions, endpoints to serve online predictions and batch prediction for large jobs, feature management, model evaluation and explanations, and model monitoring to detect drift.",
   "Vertex AI is also the home of Google Cloud's generative AI tools, including access to Gemini and other foundation models, Model Garden and Vertex AI Studio, covered in a later lesson. For the exam, remember: AutoML for custom models with little expertise, custom training for full control, and Vertex AI as the platform that unifies the ML lifecycle."
  ],
  terms: [["Vertex AI", "Google Cloud's unified platform for building, deploying and managing ML and generative AI."], ["AutoML", "Training a custom model on your own data with little or no code; the service handles model design and tuning."], ["Custom training", "Training with your own code and framework for full control over the model."], ["MLOps", "Practices and tools for deploying, monitoring and maintaining ML models in production."]],
  example: "A furniture maker has 20,000 photos of chair joints labeled good or faulty but no ML team. It uses AutoML image training in Vertex AI, deploys the model to an endpoint, and the factory camera system calls it for every chair. Later, a hired data scientist uses custom training to build a more specialized model.",
  tip: "Own labeled data plus little ML expertise points to AutoML. Unique, differentiating problem plus skilled data scientists points to custom training. Both run on Vertex AI.",
  check: [["When is AutoML a better fit than custom training?", "When a team has its own labeled data but limited ML expertise and needs a good custom model quickly."], ["Name three MLOps capabilities in Vertex AI.", "Any three of: pipelines, model registry, endpoints for serving, batch prediction, model monitoring, evaluation and explanations, feature management."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Choosing an AI approach: pre-trained API, BigQuery ML, AutoML or custom model",
  body: [
   "Google Cloud offers several ways to add AI, and the exam frequently asks which one fits a scenario. The choice balances four things: how common or unique the problem is, the data you have, the skills on the team and how quickly you need results.",
   "Pre-trained APIs, such as Vision, Natural Language, Speech-to-Text and Translation, and ready-to-use generative models, need no training at all. Choose them when the task is common (reading text in images, translating, transcribing, detecting sentiment) and the team wants results immediately without ML expertise. The trade-off is limited customization.",
   "BigQuery ML suits structured business data that already lives in BigQuery, when analysts know SQL. Typical tasks are forecasting, classification such as churn prediction, and customer segmentation. It avoids moving data and needs no new programming skills.",
   "AutoML in Vertex AI suits problems specific to your business, such as recognizing your own products or classifying your own documents, when you have labeled data but little ML expertise. It produces a custom model with much less effort than writing your own training code.",
   "Custom training in Vertex AI suits unique, complex or strategically important problems where you need full control, and you have data scientists and time. It offers the most flexibility and potential differentiation at the highest cost in skill and effort.",
   "A useful way to think about it is a ladder of effort. Start with the simplest option that meets the need, and move up only when it cannot. A company might begin with the Translation API, move to adapting translations with its own glossary, and build a custom model only if the business case justifies it. For generative AI there is a similar ladder: prompt a foundation model, ground it in company data, tune it, and only rarely train a model from scratch. Also consider buying a finished solution, such as a contact center AI product, rather than building."
  ],
  terms: [["Build vs buy", "Deciding whether to develop a solution in-house or use an existing product or service."], ["Customization", "How much a model can be adapted to an organization's specific data and needs."], ["Time to value", "How quickly an approach starts delivering business results."], ["Tuning", "Adapting a pre-trained or foundation model with additional examples for a specific task."]],
  example: "A retailer uses the Translation API to translate its website, BigQuery ML to forecast weekly demand from sales tables, AutoML to recognize its own products in shelf photos, and a custom model built by its data science team for its core pricing engine.",
  tip: "Read the clues: 'no ML expertise, common task' is a pre-trained API; 'SQL analysts, data in BigQuery' is BigQuery ML; 'own labeled data, little expertise' is AutoML; 'unique, full control, data scientists' is custom training.",
  check: [["A team of SQL analysts wants to segment customers using data in BigQuery. Which approach?", "BigQuery ML, using a clustering model created with SQL."], ["Why start with the simplest AI option that meets the need?", "It delivers value fastest with the least cost and skill; more complex options are justified only when simpler ones cannot meet the requirement."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Generative AI on Google Cloud: Gemini models, Vertex AI Model Garden and Vertex AI Studio",
  body: [
   "Generative AI lets organizations create text, images, code and more from natural language prompts. Google Cloud provides access to Google's own foundation models and to many others, along with tools to test, customize and deploy them securely for business use.",
   "Gemini is Google's family of multimodal foundation models. Multimodal means they can understand and work with several types of input, such as text, images, audio, video and code, in the same request. Businesses use them to summarize documents, answer questions, draft content, extract information, write and explain code, and power chat assistants. Different model sizes trade capability against speed and cost. Google also offers specialized models, for example for image generation, and model names and versions change often, so focus on capabilities rather than version numbers.",
   "Vertex AI Model Garden is a catalog where you can discover, compare and deploy models: Google's first-party models, models from partners, and open models. A team can evaluate several options for a task and choose the best balance of quality, cost and control, including open models it can host itself.",
   "Vertex AI Studio is a console environment for prototyping with generative models. You can write and refine prompts, adjust settings, compare responses, and then get code to call the model from an application. From there, models can be customized: prompt design, grounding in your own data, or tuning with examples.",
   "Enterprises need more than a clever model. Vertex AI provides security controls, data governance and responsible AI features such as safety filters. Google Cloud states that customer prompts and data used with its enterprise generative AI services are not used to train Google's models without permission. Gemini is also built into other Google products, such as Google Workspace for productivity and Gemini for Google Cloud to help people build and operate on the platform.",
   "Prompt quality matters: clear instructions, context, a role, the desired format and examples usually produce better results. This is called prompt engineering."
  ],
  terms: [["Gemini", "Google's family of multimodal foundation models."], ["Multimodal", "Able to process more than one type of data, such as text, images and audio, together."], ["Vertex AI Model Garden", "A catalog in Vertex AI for discovering, testing and deploying Google, partner and open models."], ["Prompt engineering", "Designing inputs to a generative model to get more useful and reliable outputs."]],
  example: "An insurance company uses Vertex AI Studio to test prompts that summarize long claim files into a one-page brief. After comparing two models from Model Garden on quality and cost, it deploys the chosen Gemini model behind an internal app, with safety filters and access controls in place.",
  tip: "Model Garden is for finding and choosing models; Vertex AI Studio is for testing prompts and prototyping; Gemini is the model family. Multimodal means text plus images, audio or video in the same model.",
  check: [["What does 'multimodal' mean for a model such as Gemini?", "It can take in and reason over several data types, such as text, images, audio and video, in the same request."], ["Where would a team compare Google, partner and open models before choosing one?", "Vertex AI Model Garden."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Grounding, agents and AI-powered search and conversation for business",
  body: [
   "Foundation models are trained on huge amounts of public data up to a point in time. On their own, they do not know a company's internal policies, product catalog or yesterday's prices, and when they lack information they may produce fluent but incorrect answers, called hallucinations. Business uses of generative AI therefore depend on connecting models to the right data and actions.",
   "Grounding means connecting a model's responses to trusted, verifiable sources, so answers are based on real information. A common technique is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG): when a user asks a question, the system first searches a knowledge source, such as company documents, a database or the web, retrieves the most relevant passages, and gives them to the model along with the question. The model then answers using that content and can cite it. Grounding improves accuracy, keeps answers current without retraining the model, and makes responses easier to check.",
   "Enterprise search applies the same ideas to finding information. Instead of matching keywords, AI-powered search understands the meaning of a question and can return a direct, summarized answer from a company's websites, documents and data, with links to sources. Google Cloud provides services in Vertex AI to build this kind of search and conversational experience over your own content without building the retrieval system yourself.",
   "An AI agent goes a step further. An agent uses a model to reason about a goal, plan steps and take actions through tools and APIs, such as looking up an order, updating a ticket or booking an appointment, then checks the result. Customer service agents, employee help desks and coding assistants are common examples. Because agents act, they need clear limits, permissions and human oversight for sensitive actions.",
   "Conversational AI for contact centers combines these pieces: understanding what a customer says, answering from trusted content, handing off to people with a summary, and helping human agents with suggested replies."
  ],
  terms: [["Hallucination", "A confident but incorrect or made-up output from a generative model."], ["Grounding", "Connecting model outputs to trusted sources so responses are based on real information."], ["Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)", "Retrieving relevant content from a knowledge source and giving it to the model with the question."], ["AI agent", "A system that uses a model to plan and take actions through tools to achieve a goal."]],
  example: "An airline builds a customer assistant grounded in its current baggage and refund policies. When a traveler asks about a delayed flight, the agent retrieves the right policy, checks the booking through an API, and offers rebooking options, handing off to a human for unusual cases.",
  tip: "When a scenario describes made-up or outdated answers from a chatbot about company information, the fix is grounding the model in trusted company data, not adding more compute.",
  check: [["How does retrieval-augmented generation reduce hallucinations?", "It retrieves relevant trusted content and gives it to the model, so the answer is based on that content rather than only on the model's training data."], ["What makes an AI agent different from a chatbot that only answers questions?", "An agent plans and takes actions through tools and APIs to complete tasks, not just generating text."]]
 },
 {
  t: "AI infrastructure: GPUs and Tensor Processing Units (TPUs)",
  body: [
   "Training and running modern machine learning models, especially large deep learning and generative models, requires enormous amounts of computation, mostly large matrix and vector math. General-purpose CPUs can do this work, but slowly. Specialized hardware called accelerators speeds it up dramatically, and access to accelerators at scale is one reason organizations use the cloud for AI.",
   "Graphics processing units (GPUs) were originally designed to render images, which also involves massive parallel math. They turned out to be excellent for ML, and they are widely supported by frameworks such as PyTorch and TensorFlow. Google Cloud offers several types of GPU that you can attach to Compute Engine VMs, use in GKE clusters or use through Vertex AI training and prediction.",
   "Tensor Processing Units (TPUs) are custom chips that Google designed specifically for machine learning. They are application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs), meaning they are built for one kind of workload rather than general computing. Google developed TPUs to run its own AI services, such as Search and Translate, efficiently, and it uses them to train its Gemini models. In Google Cloud, TPUs are available through Vertex AI, GKE and Compute Engine, and they can be connected in large groups, called pods, for training very large models.",
   "Which to use depends on the model, framework and cost. GPUs are very flexible and broadly supported. TPUs can offer strong performance and cost efficiency for large-scale training and serving of models that are suited to them. Many teams try both. For business leaders, the key point is that Google Cloud offers a range of AI infrastructure, including its own TPUs, without the need to buy and run expensive specialized hardware.",
   "You rarely need accelerators when using pre-trained APIs or generative models through Vertex AI, because Google runs that infrastructure for you. Accelerators matter when you train or host your own models."
  ],
  terms: [["Accelerator", "Specialized hardware, such as a GPU or TPU, that speeds up ML computation."], ["GPU", "Graphics processing unit: a processor with many parallel cores, widely used for ML."], ["TPU", "Tensor Processing Unit: Google's custom ASIC designed for machine learning workloads."], ["ASIC", "Application-specific integrated circuit: a chip designed for one type of task."]],
  example: "A biotech startup needs to train a large protein model for a few weeks. Instead of buying hardware, it rents TPUs through Vertex AI for the training run, then serves the finished model on smaller GPU instances, paying only while each is in use.",
  tip: "TPUs are Google's custom-designed ML chips. If a question asks what Google built specifically to accelerate machine learning, the answer is TPUs, not GPUs.",
  check: [["What is a TPU?", "A Tensor Processing Unit, Google's custom ASIC designed to accelerate machine learning training and inference."], ["Why do you rarely need to think about accelerators when using a pre-trained API?", "Google runs and manages the underlying infrastructure; you just call the API."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Why modernize: benefits of moving infrastructure and applications to the cloud",
  body: [
   "Many organizations run applications on infrastructure that is expensive to maintain, slow to change and hard to scale: aging servers, software nearing end of support, and manual processes. Modernization means updating infrastructure and applications so they can take advantage of the cloud. It is a central theme of this exam section.",
   "Infrastructure modernization usually starts with moving workloads from on-premises data centers to cloud infrastructure. The benefits are those of the cloud in general: no hardware to buy or refresh, capacity on demand, global locations, built-in redundancy, and pay-as-you-go pricing. Operations teams stop spending time on racking servers and replacing disks and can focus on automation and reliability.",
   "Application modernization goes further by changing how applications are built and run. It can mean adopting managed services, such as moving a database to Cloud SQL, packaging applications in containers, breaking monoliths into microservices, using serverless platforms, and automating build and deployment with continuous integration and delivery (CI/CD). The payoff is speed: teams can release small changes often and safely, scale parts of an application independently and recover faster from failures.",
   "Modernization also reduces risk and technical debt. Technical debt is the accumulated cost of shortcuts and outdated technology that make future changes harder. Unsupported operating systems, for example, stop receiving security patches. Moving to supported, managed platforms addresses that.",
   "Not every workload should be modernized in the same way or at the same time. Organizations usually assess their portfolio first, looking at business value, technical fit, dependencies and cost, then choose an approach per application, from simply retiring it to rebuilding it. Google Cloud provides tools such as Migration Center to discover existing servers, estimate costs and plan the move. The next lesson covers the common migration approaches."
  ],
  terms: [["Modernization", "Updating infrastructure and applications to use cloud capabilities such as managed services, containers and automation."], ["Technical debt", "The future cost created by outdated technology or shortcuts that make change harder."], ["CI/CD", "Continuous integration and continuous delivery: automating build, test and deployment of code."], ["Migration Center", "A Google Cloud tool for discovering current infrastructure, assessing it and planning a migration."]],
  example: "A logistics company's route-planning system runs on servers whose operating system has reached end of support. It moves the system to Compute Engine to remove the hardware risk, then gradually rebuilds the most-changed parts as containers on GKE, cutting release time from a month to a day.",
  tip: "Infrastructure modernization changes where things run; application modernization changes how they are built and delivered. The biggest agility gains come from the second.",
  check: [["What is technical debt, and how can modernization reduce it?", "The cost of outdated technology and shortcuts that slow change; moving to supported, managed platforms and modern designs reduces it."], ["Why assess the application portfolio before migrating?", "To choose the right approach for each application based on value, fit, dependencies and cost, rather than treating all of them the same way."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Migration approaches: retire, retain, rehost (lift and shift), replatform, refactor and reimagine",
  body: [
   "When organizations plan a cloud migration, they choose an approach for each application. The industry often describes these as the 'Rs'. Google Cloud's training uses terms such as lift and shift, move and improve, and refactor or rebuild; the exam expects you to match each approach to a situation and understand the trade-off between speed and benefit.",
   "Retire means shutting down an application that is no longer needed. Assessments often find unused or duplicate systems; retiring them saves money and reduces the migration's size. Retain means keeping an application where it is for now, for example because of a recent hardware investment, a regulation or a dependency that is not ready to move.",
   "Rehost, or lift and shift, moves an application to the cloud as it is, typically from on-premises servers to Compute Engine VMs, with no code changes. It is the fastest approach and is common when a data center must be closed quickly. It delivers some benefits, such as no hardware to manage, but few cloud-native ones. Tools such as Migrate to Virtual Machines help automate it.",
   "Replatform, or move and improve, makes targeted changes to gain cloud benefits without re-architecting. Examples include moving a self-managed database to Cloud SQL, or packaging an application in a container to run on GKE. It balances effort and benefit.",
   "Refactor, or re-architect, changes the application's code and design, for example breaking a monolith into microservices or making it serverless. It takes the most effort but gives the most agility, scalability and cost efficiency. Reimagine, or rebuild, goes further by redesigning the business process or product itself, often replacing the old application entirely, sometimes with a SaaS product (repurchase).",
   "Most organizations use several approaches at once. A common path is to rehost quickly to exit a data center, then modernize the most important applications over time."
  ],
  terms: [["Rehost (lift and shift)", "Moving an application to the cloud without changing it, usually onto VMs."], ["Replatform (move and improve)", "Making targeted changes, such as adopting a managed database, while migrating."], ["Refactor", "Changing an application's code and architecture to be cloud native."], ["Retire", "Decommissioning an application that is no longer needed instead of migrating it."]],
  example: "An insurer must leave its data center in six months. It retires 15 unused applications, rehosts 60 onto Compute Engine, replatforms its policy database to Cloud SQL, retains a mainframe for now, and plans to refactor its customer portal into microservices over the next year.",
  tip: "Deadline and no time for changes: rehost. Small changes to use managed services: replatform. Rewrite for cloud-native benefits: refactor. Unused: retire. Not moving yet: retain.",
  check: [["Which approach is fastest but gives the fewest cloud-native benefits?", "Rehost (lift and shift)."], ["An app moves to Compute Engine and its database moves to Cloud SQL without code changes. Which approach?", "Replatform (move and improve)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Virtual machines with Compute Engine: machine types, managed instance groups and autoscaling",
  body: [
   "Compute Engine is Google Cloud's infrastructure as a service (IaaS) offering for virtual machines (VMs). A VM behaves like a physical server: you choose an operating system image (Linux or Windows), a machine type and disks, and you have full control over the software installed. Compute Engine is the usual landing place for lift-and-shift migrations and for workloads that need a specific OS, kernel settings or licensed software.",
   "Machine types define how much virtual CPU (vCPU) and memory a VM has. Google Cloud organizes them into families: general-purpose for most workloads, compute-optimized for CPU-heavy work, memory-optimized for large in-memory databases, and accelerator-optimized with GPUs for ML and graphics. If no predefined type fits, custom machine types let you choose the exact number of vCPUs and amount of memory, which avoids paying for resources you do not need. You can attach persistent disks, which keep data independently of the VM, in several performance levels.",
   "A single VM is a single point of failure and cannot grow to meet demand. Managed instance groups (MIGs) solve this. You define an instance template, which describes the VM configuration, and the MIG creates and manages a group of identical VMs from it. MIGs provide autoscaling, adding VMs when load rises (for example on CPU utilization or requests per second) and removing them when load falls. They provide autohealing, recreating VMs that fail a health check. They support rolling updates to deploy a new template gradually. A regional MIG spreads VMs across zones for higher availability.",
   "MIGs are usually placed behind a Cloud Load Balancing load balancer, which spreads traffic across healthy VMs, and can be global so users reach the nearest region.",
   "Compute Engine charges per second of use for most VMs, and offers ways to lower cost, such as sustained use discounts, committed use discounts and Spot VMs, covered in the operations section. The free tier includes a small e2-micro VM in some US regions, which is enough for a first lab."
  ],
  terms: [["Compute Engine", "Google Cloud's IaaS service for running virtual machines."], ["Machine type", "The vCPU and memory configuration of a VM, predefined or custom."], ["Managed instance group (MIG)", "A group of identical VMs created from a template with autoscaling, autohealing and rolling updates."], ["Instance template", "A reusable definition of a VM's configuration used to create VMs in a MIG."]],
  example: "A ticketing site runs its web servers in a regional managed instance group behind a global load balancer. During a sale, autoscaling grows the group from 4 to 40 VMs across three zones, and when one VM crashes, autohealing replaces it without anyone being paged.",
  tip: "Automatic scaling and replacement of failed VMs points to a managed instance group. Full control of the OS points to Compute Engine rather than serverless options.",
  check: [["What does autohealing do in a managed instance group?", "It recreates VMs that fail their health check, so the group returns to a healthy state automatically."], ["When would you use a custom machine type?", "When no predefined type matches the vCPU and memory a workload needs, to avoid paying for unused resources."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Keeping existing platforms: Google Cloud VMware Engine and Bare Metal Solution",
  body: [
   "Some workloads cannot easily be moved to standard cloud VMs or rewritten. They may depend on a particular virtualization platform, specialized hardware, or licensing terms tied to physical servers. Google Cloud offers two services that let organizations move these workloads to the cloud while keeping their existing platform.",
   "Google Cloud VMware Engine runs a complete VMware environment, including the vSphere hypervisor, vSAN storage and NSX networking, as a managed service on dedicated infrastructure in Google Cloud regions. Many enterprises already run hundreds or thousands of VMs on VMware in their data centers, with established tools, processes and staff skills. With VMware Engine, they can move those VMs to Google Cloud with little or no change, using familiar VMware tools, often much faster than converting each VM. Google manages the underlying infrastructure, while the customer continues to manage its VMs. Once in Google Cloud, the workloads can connect to native services such as BigQuery and Cloud Storage and be modernized gradually.",
   "Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers, not virtual machines, located in or near Google Cloud regions and connected to them with low-latency links. It is designed for specialized workloads that must run on bare metal, most notably Oracle databases, which have licensing and support requirements that make them difficult to run on some virtualized platforms. The customer gets its own hardware while applications running elsewhere in Google Cloud can reach the database quickly.",
   "These services fit the rehost and retain-and-extend strategies. They help an organization exit its data centers on a deadline without rewriting critical systems, and they offer a stepping stone: first move the platform, then modernize the applications over time.",
   "For the exam: existing VMware environment, same tools and skills, fastest move, choose Google Cloud VMware Engine. Specialized workloads that need dedicated physical servers, such as Oracle databases, choose Bare Metal Solution."
  ],
  terms: [["Google Cloud VMware Engine", "A managed service running a native VMware environment in Google Cloud."], ["Bare Metal Solution", "Dedicated physical servers near Google Cloud regions for specialized workloads such as Oracle databases."], ["Hypervisor", "Software that creates and runs virtual machines on physical hardware."], ["Bare metal", "A physical server used directly, without a virtualization layer shared with others."]],
  example: "A retailer with 2,000 VMware VMs moves them to Google Cloud VMware Engine in a few months, keeping its vCenter tools. Its Oracle-based order system moves to Bare Metal Solution next to the region, and new analytics built in BigQuery read from both.",
  tip: "The words 'VMware', 'existing tools and processes' and 'fast migration' point to Google Cloud VMware Engine. 'Oracle' or 'dedicated physical hardware' point to Bare Metal Solution.",
  check: [["What is the main benefit of Google Cloud VMware Engine for a VMware customer?", "It can move existing VMs to Google Cloud quickly without converting them, keeping VMware tools, processes and skills."], ["Why might an Oracle database run on Bare Metal Solution rather than a VM?", "Because of licensing and support requirements that favor dedicated physical servers."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Containers: what they are and why they make applications portable",
  body: [
   "A container is a lightweight, standalone package that includes an application's code together with everything it needs to run: the runtime, libraries, system tools and settings. Containers solve a long-standing problem in software, summed up as 'it works on my machine'. Because the container carries its dependencies, the application behaves the same on a developer's laptop, in a test environment, in a data center or in any cloud.",
   "Containers are often compared with virtual machines. A VM virtualizes the hardware and includes a full guest operating system for each application, so it is relatively large and slow to start. Containers virtualize at the operating system level: many containers share the host machine's OS kernel while staying isolated from one another. That makes them much smaller, faster to start (often in seconds or less) and more efficient, since more of them fit on the same hardware.",
   "A container image is the read-only template, built from a set of instructions (for example a Dockerfile), and a container is a running instance of it. Images are stored in a registry, such as Google Cloud's Artifact Registry, from where they can be pulled and run anywhere. Because images are versioned and immutable, teams can deploy exactly what they tested and roll back easily.",
   "These properties make containers a foundation of application modernization. They fit microservices, where each service is packaged and deployed independently. They support consistent CI/CD pipelines. And they enable hybrid and multicloud strategies, because the same image can run in different environments.",
   "Running a few containers is simple; running thousands across many machines needs orchestration: scheduling containers onto servers, restarting failed ones, scaling and networking them. Kubernetes is the open source standard for that, and Google Kubernetes Engine is Google Cloud's managed version. Cloud Run runs containers without any cluster to manage."
  ],
  terms: [["Container", "A lightweight package of an application and its dependencies that runs consistently in any environment."], ["Container image", "A read-only, versioned template from which containers are started."], ["Artifact Registry", "Google Cloud's service for storing and managing container images and other build artifacts."], ["Orchestration", "Automated scheduling, scaling, networking and healing of many containers across machines."]],
  example: "A development team packages its booking service as a container image. The same image runs on developers' laptops, in the test environment and in production on GKE, so a bug found in testing reproduces exactly and a fix is deployed by pushing a new image version.",
  tip: "Portability and consistency across environments are the key container benefits. Containers share the host kernel, so they are lighter and faster to start than VMs, which each carry a full guest OS.",
  check: [["Why do containers start faster than VMs?", "They share the host operating system kernel instead of booting a full guest OS."], ["What is the difference between a container image and a container?", "The image is the read-only template; a container is a running instance of that image."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE): managed Kubernetes in Standard and Autopilot modes",
  body: [
   "Kubernetes is an open source platform for orchestrating containers: it decides where containers run, keeps the desired number running, restarts failed ones, scales them, and connects them with networking and load balancing. It began as a Google project based on Google's experience running containers internally, and it is now the industry standard, available in every major cloud and on-premises.",
   "In Kubernetes, you describe the desired state, for example 'run five copies of this container image and expose them on port 443', and Kubernetes continuously works to make reality match. A cluster has a control plane that makes decisions and nodes, the machines that run the workloads. Containers run inside pods, the smallest deployable unit.",
   "Running Kubernetes yourself is complex: installing and upgrading the control plane, securing it, and managing nodes. Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) is Google Cloud's managed Kubernetes service. Google runs the control plane, handles upgrades and integrates the cluster with Google Cloud networking, load balancing, logging, monitoring and security.",
   "GKE offers two modes of operation. In Standard mode, you manage the nodes: you choose machine types, configure node pools and pay for the node VMs whether or not they are fully used. This gives more control and flexibility. In Autopilot mode, Google also manages the nodes and underlying infrastructure, applies best-practice security settings, and you are billed for the CPU, memory and storage your pods request. Autopilot reduces operational work and wasted capacity, so it is often recommended for teams that want Kubernetes without managing infrastructure.",
   "GKE fits containerized applications that need the power of Kubernetes: many microservices, complex networking, stateful workloads, batch jobs or a desire to use the Kubernetes ecosystem and keep workloads portable. For simpler stateless services, Cloud Run may be easier. GKE Enterprise extends GKE management to clusters outside Google Cloud."
  ],
  terms: [["Kubernetes", "An open source system for automating deployment, scaling and management of containers."], ["GKE", "Google Kubernetes Engine, Google Cloud's managed Kubernetes service."], ["Autopilot", "A GKE mode where Google manages nodes and infrastructure and billing is based on pod resource requests."], ["Pod", "The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, containing one or more containers."]],
  example: "An e-commerce company runs 40 microservices on GKE. It chooses Autopilot so its small platform team does not have to size or patch nodes, and pays for the resources its pods request. During a sale, Kubernetes scales the checkout service out while leaving the rest unchanged.",
  tip: "Want Kubernetes without managing nodes: GKE Autopilot. Want control over node configuration: GKE Standard. Remember that Kubernetes itself is open source and started at Google.",
  check: [["What does Google manage in GKE Autopilot that the customer manages in Standard?", "The nodes and underlying infrastructure, including their sizing, scaling and security configuration."], ["What is desired state in Kubernetes?", "A declaration of what should be running; Kubernetes continuously acts to make the actual state match it."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Serverless computing: Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions and App Engine",
  body: [
   "Serverless computing lets developers run code without provisioning or managing servers. The platform handles infrastructure, operating system patching, scaling and availability. Applications scale automatically with demand, often down to zero when idle, and you pay for the resources used while handling requests or events. Servers still exist, but they are entirely the provider's concern. Serverless lets small teams deliver quickly and avoids paying for idle capacity.",
   "Cloud Run is a fully managed platform for running containers. You provide a container image, or source code that Cloud Run builds into one, and it gives you an HTTPS endpoint. It scales the number of instances up and down with incoming requests, including to zero, and bills for the resources used. Because it runs standard containers, you can use any language or library, and the same image could run on GKE or elsewhere. It suits web applications, APIs, microservices and jobs that run to completion.",
   "Cloud Run functions (formerly Cloud Functions) runs single-purpose pieces of code, written in supported languages, in response to events: an HTTP request, a new file in a Cloud Storage bucket, a message published to Pub/Sub, or a change in a database. You write just the function; the platform handles everything else. It is ideal for glue code and lightweight automation, such as resizing an uploaded image or sending a notification when an order is placed.",
   "App Engine is Google Cloud's original platform as a service for web applications. Developers deploy code in supported languages and App Engine manages the runtime, scaling and versions, including traffic splitting between versions. It is still used by many applications, while Cloud Run is often the choice for new containerized services.",
   "Serverless is not ideal for everything. Workloads that need full control of the operating system, special hardware, long-running processes that must always be on, or very specific networking may fit VMs or GKE better."
  ],
  terms: [["Serverless", "Running code without managing servers, with automatic scaling and pay-per-use pricing."], ["Cloud Run", "A managed serverless platform for running containers that scales automatically, including to zero."], ["Cloud Run functions", "Event-driven serverless functions that run in response to triggers such as HTTP requests or new files."], ["Scale to zero", "Running no instances, and incurring no compute cost, when there is no traffic."]],
  example: "A news site runs its API on Cloud Run so it scales up when a big story breaks and down to zero overnight. When a journalist uploads a photo to Cloud Storage, a Cloud Run function automatically creates thumbnails. The team never patches a server.",
  tip: "Containerized web app or API with no infrastructure to manage and scale to zero: Cloud Run. Small piece of code triggered by an event: Cloud Run functions. Code-based web app platform: App Engine.",
  check: [["What makes a service serverless?", "No servers to manage, automatic scaling (often to zero) and pay-per-use billing."], ["Which serverless option would you use to run code each time a message arrives in Pub/Sub?", "Cloud Run functions, triggered by the Pub/Sub event."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Choosing compute for a workload: VMs vs containers vs serverless",
  body: [
   "Google Cloud gives you a spectrum of compute options. At one end, you control everything and manage everything; at the other, you manage almost nothing but accept the platform's conventions. The exam frequently gives a workload description and asks which option fits, so it helps to have a clear decision process.",
   "Choose virtual machines on Compute Engine when you need full control over the operating system, kernel or installed software; when you are lifting and shifting an existing application without changes; when software licensing requires it; or when the workload needs specific hardware configurations. The trade-off is that you patch, secure and scale the OS yourself, though managed instance groups help.",
   "Choose containers on GKE when you have containerized applications that benefit from Kubernetes: many cooperating microservices, complex networking or deployment strategies, stateful workloads, or a desire for portability across clouds and on-premises using the Kubernetes standard. GKE Autopilot removes node management but you still work with Kubernetes concepts.",
   "Choose serverless when you want the least operational work. Cloud Run is a strong default for stateless containerized web apps, APIs and jobs, especially with variable or spiky traffic, because it scales to zero. Cloud Run functions suits small event-driven tasks. App Engine suits code-based web apps.",
   "Also consider the specialist options: Google Cloud VMware Engine for existing VMware estates, and Bare Metal Solution for workloads that need dedicated physical servers.",
   "A practical rule is to use the most managed option that meets the requirements. More management by Google means less undifferentiated work and often lower cost for variable traffic, leaving teams free to focus on features. Move toward VMs only when you need the control they give. Many organizations use all three: VMs for legacy systems, GKE for complex platforms and Cloud Run for new services."
  ],
  terms: [["Compute spectrum", "The range from IaaS VMs (most control) to serverless (least management)."], ["Stateless", "An application that stores no session data locally, so any instance can handle any request."], ["Operational overhead", "The work needed to run, patch, scale and secure infrastructure."], ["Portability", "The ability to run the same workload in different environments."]],
  example: "A company modernizing three systems keeps its licensed ERP on Compute Engine VMs, moves its 30-service order platform to GKE Autopilot, and builds a new partner API on Cloud Run because traffic is unpredictable and the team is small.",
  tip: "Use the most managed service that meets the need. Full OS control or unchanged legacy app: Compute Engine. Kubernetes features and portability: GKE. Stateless, spiky, minimal operations: Cloud Run.",
  check: [["A stateless API gets unpredictable traffic and the team wants no infrastructure work. Which option?", "Cloud Run."], ["Give two reasons to choose Compute Engine over serverless.", "Need for full OS control, an unchanged lift-and-shift, licensing requirements, or special hardware configurations (any two)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Monoliths vs microservices and application modernization",
  body: [
   "A monolithic application is built and deployed as a single unit. All its features, such as user accounts, catalog, cart, payments and notifications, live in one codebase and run as one process or package. Monoliths are simple to start with, but as they grow they become hard to change: a small update requires testing and redeploying the whole application, one bug or overloaded feature can bring everything down, and scaling means copying the entire application even if only one part is busy. Large teams working in one codebase also slow one another down.",
   "A microservices architecture splits an application into small, independent services, each responsible for one business capability and owned by a small team. Services communicate over well-defined APIs or through messaging such as Pub/Sub. Each service can be developed, deployed and scaled independently, and can even use a different language or database suited to its job. If the recommendation service fails, customers can still check out.",
   "Microservices bring their own costs: more moving parts to monitor, network calls between services, distributed data and more complex testing. They pay off for large, fast-changing applications and multiple teams, not necessarily for small, simple ones. Containers, Kubernetes, Cloud Run, API management and observability tools make them practical.",
   "Organizations rarely rewrite a monolith all at once. A common approach, sometimes called the strangler pattern, gradually carves features out into new services behind an API or load balancer, while the old application keeps running, until the monolith is small enough to retire.",
   "Modernization also includes practices. Continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) automate building, testing and deploying each service. Infrastructure as code defines environments in version-controlled files. Together with DevOps culture, these let teams release small changes frequently and safely, which is the real business goal of modernization."
  ],
  terms: [["Monolith", "An application built and deployed as a single unit."], ["Microservices", "An architecture of small, independently deployable services that communicate through APIs."], ["Strangler pattern", "Gradually replacing parts of a legacy system with new services until the old system can be retired."], ["Infrastructure as code", "Defining and managing infrastructure through version-controlled configuration files."]],
  example: "An online retailer's monolith needs a full weekend release for any change. The team first moves the product search feature into a separate service on Cloud Run behind the same URL, then the cart, then payments. Each team now releases its own service several times a week.",
  tip: "Independent deployment, independent scaling and fault isolation are the key microservices benefits. The trade-off is more operational complexity, so they suit large, fast-changing applications.",
  check: [["Give two problems of large monoliths.", "Any change requires redeploying everything, one failure can take down the whole app, and scaling means copying the entire app (any two)."], ["What is the strangler pattern?", "Gradually moving features from a legacy application into new services until the old application can be retired."]]
 },
 {
  t: "APIs and API management with Apigee",
  body: [
   "An application programming interface (API) is a defined way for one piece of software to request data or actions from another. When a mobile banking app shows your balance, it calls the bank's API; when an online shop shows shipping rates, it calls a carrier's API. APIs hide the complexity of the systems behind them and let those systems be reused in new ways.",
   "APIs are central to digital transformation. They let organizations unlock data and functions trapped in legacy systems and expose them to new apps, partners and channels without rewriting the back end. They let microservices work together. And they create new business models: a company can offer its capabilities as products that partners build on, sometimes for a fee. A retailer might publish product and inventory APIs so marketplace partners list its goods automatically.",
   "Once APIs are shared outside a single team, they need to be managed like products. API management covers security (authentication with API keys or OAuth, protection from abuse), traffic management (rate limits and quotas so one consumer cannot overload the back end), analytics (who uses which APIs and how they perform), versioning so changes do not break consumers, a developer portal with documentation and self-service sign-up, and, when needed, monetization.",
   "Apigee is Google Cloud's API management platform. It sits as a proxy layer in front of back-end services, wherever they run, on Google Cloud, on-premises or in other clouds, and applies policies for security, rate limiting, transformation and caching. It provides dashboards on API traffic and business metrics, and portals for developers. Because the proxy decouples consumers from back ends, an organization can modernize the systems behind an API without changing the apps that call it.",
   "For the exam, when a scenario involves sharing services securely with partners or developers, controlling usage with quotas, analyzing API traffic or monetizing APIs, the answer is Apigee."
  ],
  terms: [["API", "Application programming interface: a defined way for software to request data or actions from other software."], ["API management", "Securing, controlling, monitoring and publishing APIs as products."], ["Apigee", "Google Cloud's API management platform."], ["Rate limiting", "Restricting how many requests a consumer can make in a period to protect back-end systems."]],
  example: "A hospital group wants to let approved health apps show patients their appointment times. It exposes an appointments API through Apigee, which checks each app's credentials, limits request rates, logs usage for audits and offers a developer portal, while the old scheduling system behind it stays unchanged.",
  tip: "APIs unlock legacy data and enable partner ecosystems. Apigee manages them: security, quotas, analytics, developer portals and monetization.",
  check: [["How can APIs help modernize a legacy system without rewriting it?", "An API exposes the legacy system's data and functions to new apps; the back end can later be replaced without changing the consumers."], ["Name four capabilities of API management.", "Security, rate limiting and quotas, analytics, versioning, developer portals and monetization (any four)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Hybrid and multicloud with GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos)",
  body: [
   "Many organizations run applications across several environments: their own data centers, Google Cloud, other public clouds and edge locations such as stores or factories. They may do so for regulation, latency, existing investments, mergers or to avoid dependence on one provider. The challenge is that each environment has its own tools, security settings and operating processes, so teams spend time on inconsistency instead of value.",
   "Containers and Kubernetes provide a common foundation, because the same container images and Kubernetes concepts work everywhere. But running many clusters in many places still needs central management. GKE Enterprise, the evolution of what was previously called Anthos, is Google Cloud's platform for managing fleets of Kubernetes clusters across Google Cloud, on-premises data centers (on VMware or bare metal), other public clouds and edge sites.",
   "With GKE Enterprise, a platform team can see and manage all clusters from the Google Cloud console as a fleet, apply configuration and security policies consistently from a central Git repository (so every cluster has the same settings), manage service-to-service security and traffic with a service mesh, and use the same observability and deployment tools everywhere. Developers get one consistent way to build and deploy regardless of where the application runs.",
   "This supports several strategies. A company can modernize applications into containers on-premises first and move them to the cloud later with little change. It can run workloads where data must stay, such as a factory floor, while managing them centrally. It can use more than one cloud without doubling its operational tools.",
   "Other Google Cloud features support hybrid and multicloud too, such as Cloud Interconnect and Cloud VPN for private connectivity, and BigQuery Omni for analyzing data in other clouds. For the exam, the phrase 'consistent management of applications across on-premises and multiple clouds' points to GKE Enterprise."
  ],
  terms: [["GKE Enterprise", "Google Cloud's platform for managing Kubernetes clusters across Google Cloud, on-premises, other clouds and edge (formerly Anthos)."], ["Fleet", "A logical group of Kubernetes clusters managed together."], ["Service mesh", "A layer that manages secure communication, traffic and observability between services."], ["Policy as code", "Defining configuration and security policies in version-controlled files applied automatically."]],
  example: "A global manufacturer runs Kubernetes clusters in each factory for low-latency control systems, in Google Cloud for analytics and in another cloud acquired with a subsidiary. With GKE Enterprise, one platform team applies the same security policies to all clusters from a single Git repository.",
  tip: "Consistent management, policy and security across on-premises, Google Cloud and other clouds is GKE Enterprise. The older name Anthos may still appear in study materials.",
  check: [["What problem does GKE Enterprise solve?", "Inconsistent management across environments; it manages Kubernetes clusters and policies consistently across Google Cloud, on-premises, other clouds and edge."], ["Why are containers a good foundation for hybrid and multicloud?", "The same container images and Kubernetes concepts run in every environment, making workloads portable."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Core security concepts: confidentiality, integrity, availability, privacy, control and compliance",
  body: [
   "Security discussions use a small set of core terms, and the Cloud Digital Leader exam expects you to recognize them in business scenarios. Three of them form the classic CIA triad.",
   "Confidentiality means information is accessible only to those authorized to see it. A leaked customer database or an exposed storage bucket is a confidentiality failure. Controls include access management, encryption and data classification. Integrity means information is accurate and has not been altered without authorization. A tampered payment record or a changed configuration file is an integrity failure. Controls include access control, audit logs, checksums and digital signatures. Availability means systems and data are accessible when authorized users need them. A denial-of-service attack, a regional outage or ransomware that locks files are availability failures. Controls include redundancy across zones and regions, backups, DDoS protection and disaster recovery plans.",
   "Privacy is related to confidentiality but focuses on personal information and the rights of the people it describes: what is collected, why, how it is used, who it is shared with and how long it is kept. Privacy is governed by laws such as the EU's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). Security is necessary for privacy but not enough: data can be perfectly secure and still be used in ways that break privacy rules.",
   "Control refers to the customer's ability to decide how its data and systems are managed in the cloud: where data is stored, who can access it, who holds the encryption keys and whether the provider's staff can access it. Google Cloud offers features such as customer-managed keys, data location choices and Access Transparency to give customers more control.",
   "Compliance means meeting the requirements of laws, regulations, industry standards and contracts, such as GDPR, HIPAA in US healthcare or PCI DSS for card payments. Cloud providers are audited against many standards, but customers are still responsible for using services in a compliant way. Compliance is not the same as security: a compliant organization can still be breached, so compliance is a minimum rather than a goal."
  ],
  terms: [["Confidentiality", "Ensuring information is only accessible to authorized people."], ["Integrity", "Ensuring information is accurate and not altered without authorization."], ["Availability", "Ensuring systems and data are accessible when needed."], ["Compliance", "Meeting the requirements of laws, regulations, standards and contracts."]],
  example: "A payment processor encrypts card data and limits access (confidentiality), logs and signs every transaction record (integrity), runs across multiple zones with DDoS protection (availability), and undergoes a yearly PCI DSS assessment (compliance).",
  tip: "Map the incident to the property: data seen by the wrong people is confidentiality, data changed is integrity, service down is availability. Being compliant does not mean being secure.",
  check: [["Ransomware encrypts a company's files so staff cannot use them. Which CIA property is mainly affected?", "Availability (and possibly confidentiality if data was also stolen)."], ["How does privacy differ from security?", "Privacy concerns how personal data is collected and used and people's rights over it; security protects data from unauthorized access or change."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Cloud security vs on-premises security, and shared responsibility for security",
  body: [
   "In a traditional on-premises environment, the organization is responsible for every layer of security: guarding the building, securing the hardware and its supply chain, patching firmware and operating systems, protecting the network, managing identities and protecting the data. Doing all of this well requires large budgets and specialist staff that many organizations do not have.",
   "In the cloud, security is shared. Google secures the infrastructure: physical data centers, custom hardware, the global network, the storage systems and the virtualization layer. This is backed by very large investments and teams of security specialists, and by operating at a scale where threats are detected across many customers. For many organizations, this makes the foundation more secure than what they could build themselves.",
   "The customer is always responsible for its data, identities and access, and the configuration of the services it uses. How much more it manages depends on the service model. On Compute Engine (IaaS) the customer also secures the guest OS, applications and firewall rules. On serverless or PaaS, Google handles the OS and runtime. On SaaS, the customer mainly manages users, settings and data.",
   "The cloud also changes how security work is done. Resources are created in minutes by many teams, so security must be automated rather than handled only by manual review. Identity becomes the main perimeter, because services are reached over the internet rather than only from inside an office network. Configuration errors, such as public buckets or overly broad permissions, become one of the most common risks. On the positive side, the cloud offers central logging, consistent policies across the organization, and security tools that would be hard to build on-premises.",
   "Google Cloud's shared fate approach adds help on top of the split: secure-by-default settings, security blueprints and recommendations, and tools such as Security Command Center that point out risks in a customer's own configuration."
  ],
  terms: [["Shared responsibility", "The division of security duties between the cloud provider and the customer."], ["Shared fate", "Google Cloud's approach of actively helping customers secure their workloads with defaults, tools and guidance."], ["Attack surface", "All the points where an attacker could try to get into a system."], ["Identity perimeter", "Treating identity and access checks, rather than the network edge, as the main security boundary."]],
  example: "A retailer moving from its own data center no longer manages locks, cameras and hardware firmware, because Google handles those. Its security team now focuses on IAM, secure configurations, monitoring with Security Command Center and training developers, which reduces its overall risk.",
  tip: "Moving to the cloud shifts infrastructure security to Google, but data, identities, access and configuration stay with the customer in every model.",
  check: [["Name two security tasks Google handles that an on-premises organization must do itself.", "Physical data center security, hardware and supply chain security, and securing the network and virtualization layers (any two)."], ["Why does identity become more important in the cloud?", "Services are reached over the internet from anywhere, so verifying who is making each request becomes the main line of defense."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Common cloud threats: misconfiguration, compromised credentials, phishing, malware and ransomware",
  body: [
   "Knowing the common threats helps leaders invest in the right controls. Many cloud incidents are not caused by sophisticated attacks on the provider but by weaknesses on the customer's side.",
   "Misconfiguration is one of the leading causes of cloud data exposure. Examples include storage buckets made publicly readable by mistake, databases reachable from the internet, firewall rules open to all addresses, logging turned off, and users with far more permissions than they need. Because cloud resources are easy to create, mistakes can spread quickly. Defenses include secure defaults, organization policies that block risky settings, infrastructure as code with review, and tools such as Security Command Center that detect misconfigurations.",
   "Compromised credentials occur when attackers obtain passwords, API keys or service account keys, for example through reuse of passwords leaked elsewhere or keys accidentally published in code repositories. With valid credentials, attackers look like legitimate users. Defenses include strong multi-factor authentication, avoiding long-lived keys, least privilege and monitoring for unusual activity.",
   "Phishing tricks people into revealing credentials or running malicious software, usually through emails or messages that impersonate a trusted person or service. Phishing-resistant two-step verification, such as security keys, is one of the most effective defenses, together with awareness training.",
   "Malware is malicious software, such as viruses, trojans and spyware. Ransomware is malware that encrypts an organization's data and demands payment to restore it, often also threatening to publish stolen data. Defenses include patching, limiting privileges, protecting endpoints, email filtering and, crucially, backups that attackers cannot alter or delete, so data can be restored without paying.",
   "Other threats include denial-of-service attacks that overwhelm services, insider threats from employees misusing access, and attacks on software supply chains. Defense in depth, discussed in the next lesson, means no single control has to stop everything."
  ],
  terms: [["Misconfiguration", "An insecure setting, such as public access or overly broad permissions, often made by mistake."], ["Phishing", "Deceptive messages designed to trick people into revealing credentials or running malware."], ["Ransomware", "Malware that encrypts data and demands payment to restore access."], ["Compromised credentials", "Passwords, keys or tokens obtained by an attacker and used to impersonate a legitimate user."]],
  example: "A developer accidentally commits a service account key to a public code repository. Within hours, attackers use it to create VMs for cryptocurrency mining. The company revokes the key, switches to keyless authentication for its pipeline, and enables alerts on unusual resource creation.",
  tip: "Most cloud breaches trace back to customer-side issues: misconfiguration and stolen credentials. The strongest defenses are secure configuration, least privilege and phishing-resistant multi-factor authentication.",
  check: [["What is the most effective defense against losing data to ransomware?", "Protected backups that attackers cannot change or delete, allowing restore without paying, along with patching and least privilege."], ["Give two examples of cloud misconfiguration.", "A publicly readable storage bucket, a database open to the internet, firewall rules allowing all addresses, or excessive permissions (any two)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Zero trust and defense in depth",
  body: [
   "Traditional security used a castle-and-moat model: a strong perimeter firewall protected the corporate network, and anything inside was trusted. That model fits poorly today. People work from home and on the move, applications run in the cloud and are reached over the internet, and an attacker who gets inside the perimeter, for example through a phished laptop, can move around freely.",
   "Zero trust is a security model that removes implicit trust based on network location. Its motto is 'never trust, always verify'. Every request to access an application or data is authenticated and authorized based on who the user is, the security state of their device and the context of the request, such as location and time, regardless of whether it comes from the office network or a coffee shop. Access is granted with least privilege, for the specific resource, and is continuously evaluated.",
   "Google adopted this approach internally after a sophisticated attack in 2009, and called its implementation BeyondCorp. Google employees access internal applications without a traditional VPN; access decisions depend on user identity and device state. Google Cloud offers these ideas to customers, for example through Identity-Aware Proxy, which checks identity and context before allowing access to applications and VMs, and through Chrome Enterprise Premium (previously BeyondCorp Enterprise).",
   "Defense in depth is a complementary principle: use multiple layers of independent security controls so that if one fails, others still protect the asset. Layers in Google Cloud might include strong identity and two-step verification, IAM with least privilege, organization policies, network firewalls and Cloud Armor, encryption at rest and in transit, VPC Service Controls, logging and threat detection, and backups. An attacker who steals a password still faces 2SV; one who bypasses a firewall still lacks permissions; one who reaches the data still finds it encrypted and their actions logged.",
   "Together, zero trust and defense in depth replace the idea of one strong wall with continuous verification and layered protection."
  ],
  terms: [["Zero trust", "A model that grants no implicit trust based on network location and verifies every request."], ["BeyondCorp", "Google's implementation of zero trust, allowing access based on user and device rather than network."], ["Defense in depth", "Layering multiple independent security controls so one failure does not expose the system."], ["Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP)", "A Google Cloud service that checks identity and context before granting access to applications and VMs."]],
  example: "A consulting firm stops requiring a VPN for its internal apps. Each request passes through Identity-Aware Proxy, which checks the employee's identity, 2SV and whether the laptop is company-managed and up to date. A contractor's personal device is refused even with a valid password.",
  tip: "Zero trust: verify every request by identity and context, not network location. Defense in depth: several layers of controls. If a question describes 'trusting everything inside the network', it describes the old perimeter model.",
  check: [["What is the main idea of zero trust?", "No implicit trust from network location; every access request is verified using identity, device and context, with least privilege."], ["Why is defense in depth valuable?", "If one control fails or is bypassed, other independent layers still protect the data or system."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Google's secure infrastructure: data centers, custom hardware, Titan chips and the private network",
  body: [
   "Google Cloud runs on the same infrastructure that Google uses for its own services, which serve billions of users. Google designs security into every layer of this infrastructure, from the physical buildings up to the services, and publishes an overview of the design. The exam expects you to know the main layers at a high level.",
   "Physical security: Google's data centers use multiple layers of protection, such as perimeter fencing, security guards, vehicle barriers, cameras, access badges, biometric checks and restrictions so that only a small number of approved staff can enter sensitive areas. Hard drives that fail or reach end of life are securely erased or physically destroyed before leaving the site.",
   "Hardware and boot security: Google designs its own servers, network equipment and some chips, rather than relying only on off-the-shelf products. Titan is a Google-designed security chip that establishes a hardware root of trust: it verifies that a machine boots with legitimate, unmodified firmware and software. This helps protect against tampering in the supply chain or at boot time. Shielded VMs bring related protections, such as secure boot and integrity monitoring, to customers' Compute Engine VMs.",
   "Service and network security: Services within Google's infrastructure authenticate to each other cryptographically, and traffic is encrypted in transit between Google's facilities. Google operates one of the largest private networks in the world, including its own fiber and subsea cables, and customer traffic travels on it for much of its journey. At the edge, the scale of Google's network helps absorb large denial-of-service attacks.",
   "Operational security: Google employs large teams of security and privacy engineers, runs continuous threat detection, strictly limits and logs employee access to production systems, and runs a vulnerability rewards program that pays researchers for reporting flaws.",
   "Storage security: data is encrypted at rest by default, as covered in the next lesson. Together, these layers mean customers start from a strong, audited foundation, although they must still secure their own configurations and data."
  ],
  terms: [["Titan chip", "A Google-designed security chip that verifies machines boot with trusted firmware and software."], ["Hardware root of trust", "A trusted hardware component that verifies the integrity of the system from start-up."], ["Shielded VM", "A Compute Engine VM with secure boot and integrity monitoring against boot-level tampering."], ["Defense at scale", "Using the size of Google's network and security operations to detect and absorb attacks."]],
  example: "A regulator asks a bank how its cloud provider protects hardware from tampering. The bank explains that Google designs its own servers with Titan chips that verify firmware at boot, controls data center access with several layers of physical security, and destroys retired drives on site.",
  tip: "Know the layers: physical data center security, custom hardware with Titan chips, encrypted and authenticated service communication, a private global network, and strict operational controls. Customers inherit all of these.",
  check: [["What does the Titan chip do?", "It acts as a hardware root of trust, verifying that a machine boots with legitimate, unmodified firmware and software."], ["How does Google handle failed or retired storage drives?", "It securely erases them or physically destroys them before they leave the data center."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Encryption at rest and in transit, and key management with Cloud KMS",
  body: [
   "Encryption transforms readable data into ciphertext that can only be read with the right key. It protects confidentiality even if storage media or network traffic are accessed by someone unauthorized. Google Cloud applies encryption by default in two states.",
   "Encryption at rest protects stored data. All customer data stored in Google Cloud is encrypted at rest by default, with no action needed from the customer, using strong encryption (AES-256). Data is split into chunks, each encrypted with its own data encryption key, and those keys are themselves encrypted by key encryption keys managed in Google's key management systems. This layered approach is called envelope encryption.",
   "Encryption in transit protects data moving across networks. Google encrypts and authenticates data in transit between its data centers and at various layers within them, and services use TLS for connections from users and applications. Customers should also use HTTPS for their own applications and encrypted connections such as Cloud VPN for traffic from on-premises.",
   "Some organizations need more control over the keys, for regulation or internal policy. Google Cloud offers several options. Google-managed keys are the default: Google handles everything. Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) are keys the customer creates and manages in Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS). The customer controls rotation schedules, permissions and can disable or destroy a key, which makes data protected by it unreadable. Keys can be protected in hardware security modules with Cloud HSM. With Cloud External Key Manager (EKM), keys are held in a key management system outside Google, so the customer can refuse access. Customer-supplied encryption keys (CSEK), where the customer provides the key with requests, are supported by some services.",
   "More control brings more responsibility: if a customer destroys or loses its key, Google cannot recover the data. Most organizations use the default, and choose CMEK or external keys for specific compliance needs. Cloud KMS also logs key use, which helps with audits."
  ],
  terms: [["Encryption at rest", "Encrypting stored data; on by default for all customer data in Google Cloud."], ["Encryption in transit", "Encrypting data as it moves across networks, for example with TLS."], ["CMEK", "Customer-managed encryption keys: keys the customer creates and controls in Cloud KMS."], ["Cloud KMS", "Google Cloud's service for creating, managing, rotating and using cryptographic keys."]],
  example: "A financial firm's policy requires that it can revoke access to its data at any time. It creates keys in Cloud KMS with a 90-day rotation schedule and uses them as CMEK for its BigQuery datasets and Cloud Storage buckets. Disabling a key would make that data unreadable, even to services processing it.",
  tip: "Encryption at rest is on by default; customers do not need to enable it. Choose CMEK in Cloud KMS when a question stresses customer control over key rotation, disabling or destroying keys.",
  check: [["Does a customer have to turn on encryption at rest in Google Cloud?", "No. All customer data is encrypted at rest by default with Google-managed keys."], ["What is the risk of managing your own keys?", "If the key is destroyed or lost, the data it protects cannot be recovered, even by Google."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Identity and access management: principals, roles, least privilege and two-step verification",
  body: [
   "Identity and access management (IAM) answers the question: who can do what on which resource? In the cloud, where resources are reached over the internet, IAM is one of the most important security controls, and misused access is behind many incidents.",
   "In Google Cloud IAM, a principal is the identity that receives access. Principals include Google accounts for individual users, Google groups, service accounts (identities used by applications and VMs rather than people), and Cloud Identity or Google Workspace domains. Managing access through groups rather than individuals makes it easier to add and remove people as their jobs change. Organizations often manage user accounts with Cloud Identity or Google Workspace and can sync them from existing directories.",
   "Permissions allow specific actions, such as starting a VM or reading a storage object. Permissions are not granted directly; they are bundled into roles, and roles are granted to principals on a resource through an allow policy. Basic roles (Owner, Editor, Viewer) are broad and apply across all services in a project; they are not recommended for most production use. Predefined roles are created by Google for specific jobs on specific services, such as Storage Object Viewer or Compute Instance Admin. Custom roles let you define exactly the permissions you need.",
   "The principle of least privilege means giving each principal only the permissions needed for its task, on the smallest scope, for no longer than needed. In practice: prefer predefined or custom roles over basic roles, grant at the project or resource level rather than the organization when possible, use groups, review access regularly, and act on IAM recommendations that point out unused permissions. Separation of duties, where no single person controls a whole sensitive process, complements it.",
   "Authentication also matters. Two-step verification (2SV), also called multi-factor authentication, requires a second factor in addition to a password, such as a code from an app or, better, a physical security key. Security keys resist phishing because they only work with the genuine site. Administrators should always use strong 2SV."
  ],
  terms: [["Principal", "An identity that can be granted access, such as a user, group or service account."], ["Role", "A collection of permissions that can be granted to a principal on a resource."], ["Least privilege", "Granting only the permissions needed, on the narrowest scope, for only as long as needed."], ["Service account", "An identity used by applications and workloads rather than people."]],
  example: "A company replaces the Editor role it had given all developers with a group that has predefined roles for the specific services they use, in development projects only. Production access is granted to a small operations group, and every administrator uses a security key for 2SV.",
  tip: "Grant roles, not permissions, to groups rather than individuals, on the smallest scope. Basic roles (Owner, Editor, Viewer) are almost never the least-privilege answer.",
  check: [["What are the three kinds of IAM roles in Google Cloud?", "Basic (Owner, Editor, Viewer), predefined, and custom roles."], ["Why are security keys better than SMS codes for 2SV?", "Security keys resist phishing because they only authenticate to the genuine site, while codes can be tricked out of users."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Network and perimeter security: firewall rules, Cloud Armor and VPC Service Controls",
  body: [
   "Even in a zero trust world, network controls remain an important layer of defense in depth. Google Cloud provides several, each protecting against different threats.",
   "A Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) is a customer's private network in Google Cloud, spanning regions, in which resources such as VMs get internal IP addresses. VPC firewall rules and firewall policies allow or deny traffic to and from resources based on addresses, ports, protocols, network tags or service accounts. Good practice is to deny by default and allow only what is needed, for example allowing HTTPS from the internet only to the load balancer and SSH only from Identity-Aware Proxy rather than from anywhere. Firewall policies can also be applied centrally at the organization or folder level. Cloud NGFW adds more advanced inspection features.",
   "Cloud Armor protects internet-facing applications and websites that sit behind Google Cloud's external load balancers. It provides protection against distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks, using the scale of Google's edge network to absorb large volumes of traffic, and it acts as a web application firewall (WAF) with preconfigured rules for common attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting. Rules can also block or allow traffic by IP address or geographic region and limit request rates. Because it works at the edge, attacks are stopped before they reach the application.",
   "VPC Service Controls addresses a different risk: data exfiltration from Google Cloud managed services such as Cloud Storage and BigQuery. It lets you draw a service perimeter around projects and services. Requests to access data from outside the perimeter, or to copy data to resources outside it, are blocked even if the caller has valid IAM credentials. This helps contain the damage from stolen credentials or malicious insiders.",
   "Private connectivity options such as Private Google Access and Private Service Connect let workloads reach Google services without using public IP addresses, and Cloud VPN and Cloud Interconnect provide secure connections to on-premises networks."
  ],
  terms: [["VPC firewall rules", "Rules that allow or deny network traffic to and from resources in a VPC network."], ["Cloud Armor", "Google Cloud's DDoS protection and web application firewall for applications behind external load balancers."], ["Web application firewall (WAF)", "A filter that blocks common web attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting."], ["VPC Service Controls", "Service perimeters around Google Cloud services that reduce the risk of data exfiltration."]],
  example: "An online store puts its site behind a global load balancer with Cloud Armor, which absorbs a large DDoS attack and blocks SQL injection attempts. Its customer data in BigQuery sits inside a VPC Service Controls perimeter, so a stolen analyst credential cannot be used to copy tables to an outside project.",
  tip: "DDoS and web attacks against a public site: Cloud Armor. Data being copied out of managed services even with valid credentials: VPC Service Controls. Allowing or denying traffic to VMs by port and address: firewall rules.",
  check: [["Which service protects a public web app from SQL injection and DDoS at Google's edge?", "Cloud Armor."], ["What does VPC Service Controls protect against that IAM alone does not?", "Data exfiltration by someone with valid credentials, by blocking access and copying across the service perimeter."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Security operations: Security Command Center, audit logs and Google Security Operations",
  body: [
   "Security operations (SecOps) is the continuous work of detecting, investigating and responding to threats. Preventive controls are never perfect, so organizations need visibility into what is happening and the ability to react quickly. Google Cloud provides tools at several levels.",
   "Security Command Center is Google Cloud's built-in security and risk management platform. It gives a central view across an organization's projects of assets, misconfigurations (such as public buckets or open firewall ports), vulnerabilities (such as outdated software on VMs or web app flaws), and active threats (such as a VM mining cryptocurrency or unusual IAM grants). It prioritizes findings and recommends fixes, and it can check the environment against compliance benchmarks. Higher tiers add more detection capabilities.",
   "Cloud Audit Logs record who did what, where and when in Google Cloud. Admin Activity audit logs record configuration changes, such as creating a VM or changing IAM policies; they are always on and cannot be disabled. Data Access audit logs record reads and writes of user data and are mostly off by default because of their volume; you enable them where needed. System Event logs record Google-initiated changes, and Policy Denied logs record access refused by security policies. Logs are viewed in Cloud Logging's Logs Explorer and can be exported to BigQuery or other tools for long-term analysis. Audit logs answer questions like 'who deleted this instance?' and support investigations and compliance.",
   "Google Security Operations (formerly Chronicle) is a cloud-native security operations platform that combines SIEM (security information and event management) and SOAR (security orchestration, automation and response). It ingests large volumes of security telemetry from cloud, on-premises and other sources, enriches it with Google threat intelligence, including from Mandiant, detects threats, and automates response workflows.",
   "Other services help too, such as Sensitive Data Protection to discover sensitive information and Web Security Scanner for app vulnerabilities. Mandiant provides incident response and threat intelligence expertise."
  ],
  terms: [["Security Command Center", "Google Cloud's central platform for security posture, vulnerabilities, misconfigurations and threat detection."], ["Cloud Audit Logs", "Logs recording administrative actions and data access in Google Cloud."], ["SIEM", "Security information and event management: collecting and analyzing security logs to detect threats."], ["SOAR", "Security orchestration, automation and response: automating investigation and response steps."]],
  example: "Security Command Center flags that a new Cloud Storage bucket is publicly readable and that a VM shows signs of cryptomining. The security team checks the Admin Activity audit logs to see which account created the bucket and made it public, removes public access and isolates the VM.",
  tip: "Posture and threats across Google Cloud projects: Security Command Center. Who did what and when: Cloud Audit Logs (Admin Activity is always on). Enterprise-wide SIEM and SOAR: Google Security Operations.",
  check: [["Which audit logs are always on and cannot be disabled?", "Admin Activity audit logs."], ["What does Security Command Center provide?", "A central view of assets, misconfigurations, vulnerabilities and threats across an organization, with prioritized findings and recommendations."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Data residency, data sovereignty and Assured Workloads",
  body: [
   "Many organizations, especially in the public sector, healthcare and finance, must meet rules about where data is stored and who can access it. These rules come from national laws, industry regulations and customer contracts, and they are a common concern when moving to the cloud.",
   "Data residency is the physical or geographic location where data is stored and processed. In Google Cloud, you control residency mainly by choosing regions and multi-regions when you create resources, for example storing a bucket in `europe-west3` (Frankfurt). To enforce it across an organization, administrators use the resource locations organization policy constraint, which stops anyone from creating resources outside allowed locations.",
   "Data sovereignty is a broader, legal concept: data is subject to the laws of the country where it is located, and organizations and governments want assurance about which jurisdictions can compel access to it and who controls it. Sovereignty concerns include where data is stored, who holds the encryption keys, which personnel can access systems and from where, and whether operations could be affected by foreign laws. Google Cloud addresses these with data location controls, customer-managed and external encryption keys, access controls and transparency features, and, in some countries, sovereign cloud offerings delivered with local partners.",
   "Assured Workloads is a Google Cloud service that helps customers run regulated workloads by applying a set of controls for a chosen compliance regime, such as specific government, healthcare or regional requirements. When you create an Assured Workloads folder, it enforces controls such as data location restrictions, restrictions on which products can be used, and limits on which Google personnel can provide support, for example only personnel in certain countries or with certain background checks. It monitors for violations of these controls.",
   "Residency and sovereignty are part of the customer's compliance responsibility. Google provides the tools, but the customer must choose locations and configure controls correctly."
  ],
  terms: [["Data residency", "The geographic location where data is stored and processed."], ["Data sovereignty", "The principle that data is subject to the laws of the country where it is located, including control over who can access it."], ["Assured Workloads", "A Google Cloud service that applies controls, such as data location and personnel access, for regulated workloads."], ["Resource locations constraint", "An organization policy that restricts the locations where resources can be created."]],
  example: "A German public agency must keep citizen data in the EU and limit support access to EU personnel. It creates an Assured Workloads folder for the relevant EU regime, which restricts resource locations to EU regions and applies personnel access controls, and it uses external keys it holds itself.",
  tip: "Residency is about where data sits, set by choosing regions and enforced with the resource locations policy. Sovereignty adds who controls and can access it under which laws. Regulated workloads with location and personnel controls point to Assured Workloads.",
  check: [["How can an organization prevent any project from creating resources outside the EU?", "Set the resource locations organization policy constraint to EU locations at the organization node."], ["Name two controls Assured Workloads can enforce.", "Data location restrictions, restrictions on available products, and limits on which Google personnel can access or support the workload (any two)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Compliance and transparency: compliance reports, Access Transparency and Google's trust principles",
  body: [
   "Moving sensitive workloads to the cloud requires trust in the provider. Organizations need evidence that the provider meets recognized standards, clear commitments about how their data is handled, and visibility into any access by the provider's staff.",
   "Google Cloud is regularly audited by independent third parties against many international and industry standards and regulations. Examples include ISO/IEC 27001 for information security management, ISO/IEC 27017 and 27018 for cloud security and privacy, SOC 1, SOC 2 and SOC 3 reports, PCI DSS for card payments, and support for requirements such as HIPAA in US healthcare and GDPR in the EU. Customers can download certificates and audit reports from Google's Compliance Reports Manager to use in their own risk assessments and audits. Remember that the provider's certifications cover its part; customers must still use services in a compliant way.",
   "Google Cloud has published trust principles that describe its commitments to customers. In summary: customers own their data, and Google does not sell customer data or use it for advertising; Google processes customer data only according to the customer's agreement and instructions; customer data is encrypted by default; Google guards against insider access to customer data; Google does not give any government access to customer data except through a legal process, and publishes transparency reports about such requests; and Google's privacy practices are audited against international standards.",
   "Two features give customers visibility and control over provider access. Access Transparency provides near real-time logs of actions taken by Google personnel on customer content, with the reason, such as a support case the customer opened. Access Approval goes further, letting customers require explicit approval before Google personnel can access their content for supported services, with limited exceptions.",
   "These tools let customers verify, rather than simply trust, how their data is handled."
  ],
  terms: [["Compliance Reports Manager", "Google's portal for downloading certifications and third-party audit reports."], ["Access Transparency", "Logs of actions Google personnel take on customer content, with justifications."], ["Access Approval", "A feature that requires customer approval before Google personnel access customer content."], ["SOC 2 report", "An independent audit report on a service provider's controls for security, availability, confidentiality and related criteria."]],
  example: "Before approving a move to Google Cloud, a hospital's compliance officer downloads Google's ISO 27001 certificate and SOC 2 report from Compliance Reports Manager, reviews the trust principles and requires Access Transparency and Access Approval for the projects holding patient data.",
  tip: "Audit reports and certificates: Compliance Reports Manager. Logs of Google staff access: Access Transparency. Approving access before it happens: Access Approval. Provider certifications do not make the customer compliant automatically.",
  check: [["What is the difference between Access Transparency and Access Approval?", "Access Transparency logs Google personnel access after the fact with a reason; Access Approval requires the customer to approve access before it happens."], ["Name two of Google Cloud's trust commitments.", "Customers own their data; Google does not sell it or use it for ads; data is encrypted by default; government requests go through legal process (any two)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Cloud financial governance and FinOps: shared accountability for cloud cost",
  body: [
   "In a traditional data center, spending is controlled up front: finance approves a hardware purchase, and afterwards the cost is fixed. In the cloud, any engineer with permission can create resources in minutes, and costs accumulate by the second. That flexibility is valuable, but without new practices it can lead to surprise bills, idle resources and no clear idea of who spent what.",
   "Cloud financial governance is the set of policies, processes and tools that keep cloud spending aligned with business value. FinOps, short for cloud financial operations, is the widely used practice for doing this. It brings together finance, technology and business teams so that everyone shares responsibility for cost. Engineers see the cost of their decisions, finance understands the variable nature of cloud spending, and business leaders decide trade-offs between speed, quality and cost.",
   "FinOps is often described as a cycle of three phases. Inform: make costs visible and allocate them to teams, products and projects, using the resource hierarchy, labels, billing reports and dashboards. Optimize: reduce waste and get better rates, for example by removing idle resources, rightsizing over-provisioned VMs, using autoscaling, choosing appropriate storage classes and buying commitments for steady workloads. Operate: set budgets and targets, automate controls and continuously improve.",
   "A key idea is that the goal is not simply to spend less but to get the most value per dollar. Spending more on a service that brings in more revenue can be a good decision; paying for forgotten test VMs is not.",
   "Google Cloud supports FinOps with tools covered in the following lessons: the resource hierarchy for organizing projects, Cloud Billing reports and exports, budgets and alerts, quotas, labels, and recommendations from Active Assist (the Recommender) that identify idle resources and rightsizing opportunities. Organizations often set up a central team, sometimes within the cloud center of excellence, to drive these practices."
  ],
  terms: [["FinOps", "A practice that brings finance, technology and business together to manage cloud costs and maximize value."], ["Cost allocation", "Assigning cloud costs to the teams, products or projects that incur them."], ["Rightsizing", "Changing resources to the size that actually matches their workload."], ["Active Assist recommendations", "Google Cloud suggestions, such as removing idle VMs or resizing machines, to reduce cost and improve security."]],
  example: "A software company's cloud bill doubles in a quarter and nobody knows why. It forms a FinOps group, labels every project by team, gives each team a cost dashboard and budget, and acts on recommendations to delete idle VMs. Waste falls, and teams start considering cost when designing features.",
  tip: "FinOps is about shared accountability and maximizing business value, not only cutting cost. Look for answers that involve visibility and collaboration between finance and engineering.",
  check: [["What are the three FinOps phases?", "Inform (visibility and allocation), optimize (reduce waste and improve rates), and operate (set targets, automate and improve continuously)."], ["Why does cloud spending need new governance compared with on-premises?", "Because anyone with permission can create resources instantly and costs are variable, so control must be continuous rather than only at purchase time."]]
 },
 {
  t: "The Google Cloud resource hierarchy: organization, folders, projects and resources",
  body: [
   "Google Cloud organizes everything a company uses into a hierarchy. The hierarchy is the foundation for managing access, applying policies and tracking costs, so designing it well is one of the first steps in a cloud adoption.",
   "At the top is the organization node, which represents the company. It is linked to the company's Cloud Identity or Google Workspace domain, such as example.com. Having an organization node gives central visibility and control over all projects, and it means projects belong to the company, not to individual employees who may leave.",
   "Below it are folders, which group projects and other folders. Folders commonly represent departments (Finance, Marketing), teams, products or environments (Production, Development). Folders can be nested, which lets you mirror the company's structure and apply different policies to different groups.",
   "Projects are the next level and the most important unit for day-to-day work. Every Google Cloud resource belongs to exactly one project. A project is where you enable APIs and services, it is linked to a billing account that pays for its usage, and it has its own IAM policies and quotas. Each project has a project name, a globally unique project ID that cannot be changed, and a project number. Using separate projects for different applications and environments keeps them isolated, simplifies access control and makes costs easy to see.",
   "At the bottom are the resources themselves: VMs, Cloud Storage buckets, BigQuery datasets, Pub/Sub topics and so on.",
   "Policies flow downward. An IAM grant or organization policy set on the organization applies to all folders, projects and resources below it; one set on a folder applies to everything in that folder. This is covered in the next lesson. A common good practice is to set broad guardrails high in the hierarchy and grant most access at the folder or project level."
  ],
  terms: [["Organization node", "The root of the resource hierarchy, representing the company."], ["Folder", "A grouping of projects and other folders, often by department, team or environment."], ["Project", "The basic unit for enabling services, billing, IAM and grouping resources; every resource belongs to one."], ["Project ID", "A globally unique, permanent identifier for a project."]],
  example: "A retail company creates an organization node for example.com, folders for Stores, E-commerce and Corporate, and inside E-commerce separate projects for web-prod, web-dev and analytics. Each project links to the central billing account, and costs roll up clearly by folder.",
  tip: "Remember the order: organization, folders, projects, resources. Every resource lives in exactly one project, and projects are where APIs are enabled and billing is linked.",
  check: [["What is the correct order of the resource hierarchy from top to bottom?", "Organization, folders, projects, resources."], ["Why use separate projects for production and development?", "To isolate them, apply different access and policies, and see their costs separately."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Policy inheritance: IAM allow policies and organization policies through the hierarchy",
  body: [
   "One of the main benefits of the resource hierarchy is that policies are inherited. A policy set on a node applies to that node and everything beneath it. This lets administrators manage access and guardrails for thousands of resources centrally, instead of configuring each one.",
   "IAM allow policies grant roles to principals. If you grant a group the Viewer role on a folder, the group can view every project and resource in that folder, including ones created later. A resource's effective policy is the union of the policy set on it and the policies inherited from its ancestors. IAM access is additive: a policy lower in the hierarchy can grant more access, but an allow policy cannot take away access granted higher up. For example, if a user is an Editor on a folder, removing them from a project inside the folder does not stop them editing it. This is why broad roles should be granted high in the hierarchy only with great care, and why least privilege applies to where you grant as well as what you grant. Google Cloud also offers IAM deny policies, which can explicitly block certain permissions and take precedence over allow policies, for cases where central teams need firm limits.",
   "Organization policies are different. Instead of controlling who can do something, they control what can be done, for everyone, regardless of IAM roles. They are set with constraints through the Organization Policy Service. Examples include restricting which regions resources can be created in, preventing public access to Cloud Storage buckets, disabling the creation of service account keys, and restricting which external domains can be granted access. Organization policies are inherited down the hierarchy, and depending on the constraint, a lower level can be allowed to customize or merge them.",
   "Together they form the governance model: organization policies set guardrails that even project owners cannot break, and IAM decides who can act within those guardrails."
  ],
  terms: [["Policy inheritance", "Policies set on a node apply to all descendants in the hierarchy."], ["IAM allow policy", "A policy that grants roles to principals on a resource."], ["Organization policy", "A constraint that restricts how resources can be configured, regardless of who acts."], ["Additive access", "Effective permissions are the union of all grants at a resource and its ancestors."]],
  example: "A bank sets an organization policy at the organization node that blocks public access to Cloud Storage and restricts resources to two regions. Even a project owner in a development folder cannot create a public bucket or a VM in another region, while IAM still decides who can create resources at all.",
  tip: "IAM controls who can do what; organization policies control what can be configured, for everyone. IAM allow grants are additive, so a grant at a folder cannot be removed at a project below it.",
  check: [["A user has Editor on a folder. Can a project owner remove that access for one project in the folder with an allow policy?", "No. Allow policies are additive and inherited; access must be removed where it was granted (or blocked with a deny policy)."], ["How does an organization policy differ from an IAM role?", "An organization policy restricts what configurations are allowed for everyone; an IAM role grants permissions to specific principals."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Controlling costs: billing accounts, budgets and alerts, quotas, labels and billing export",
  body: [
   "Google Cloud provides several tools to see, allocate and control spending. The exam often tests what each one does and, just as importantly, what it does not do.",
   "A Cloud Billing account pays for usage. It is linked to a payment method and to one or more projects; each project is linked to one billing account at a time. Access to billing is controlled separately with roles such as Billing Account Administrator and Billing Account Viewer, so finance staff can see costs without being able to change resources.",
   "Budgets and budget alerts let you set a planned amount for a billing account, a project, a set of services or resources with certain labels, and receive notifications when actual or forecast spending reaches thresholds, such as 50%, 90% and 100%. Budgets do not automatically stop resources or cap spending. If you need automatic action, you can send budget notifications to Pub/Sub and trigger automation, for example a function that disables billing on a sandbox project.",
   "Quotas limit how much of a resource a project can use, such as the number of vCPUs in a region or API requests per minute. They exist to protect both customers and Google Cloud from unexpected spikes, runaway scripts or abuse, and they can indirectly limit spending. Some quotas can be increased by request, and administrators can lower some of them to set tighter limits.",
   "Labels are key-value pairs, such as `team=marketing` or `env=prod`, attached to resources. They appear in billing data, so costs can be grouped and filtered by team, application or environment. A consistent labeling strategy is essential for cost allocation.",
   "Billing reports in the console show spending trends and breakdowns by project, service, region and label. For deeper analysis, Cloud Billing export sends detailed usage and cost data to BigQuery, where it can be queried with SQL and visualized in Looker Studio. The Pricing Calculator estimates costs before you build."
  ],
  terms: [["Cloud Billing account", "The account that pays for Google Cloud usage, linked to projects and a payment method."], ["Budget alert", "A notification sent when actual or forecast spend reaches a threshold; it does not stop spending."], ["Quota", "A limit on how much of a resource or API a project can use."], ["Label", "A key-value pair attached to resources, used to organize and allocate costs."]],
  example: "A university gives each research group its own project with a budget and alerts at 50% and 90%. Resources carry labels for grant number, and billing data is exported to BigQuery so the finance office can charge each grant accurately each month. A sandbox project uses a Pub/Sub-triggered function to disable billing when its budget is exceeded.",
  tip: "Budgets alert, they do not cap. Quotas limit resource use. Labels allocate cost. Billing export to BigQuery enables detailed analysis. Know which tool fits each goal.",
  check: [["Will a budget automatically stop resources when it is exceeded?", "No. Budgets send alerts; automatic action requires automation, for example through Pub/Sub notifications."], ["How can a company see cloud costs by team?", "Apply labels such as team to resources (or use separate projects), then group billing reports or exported billing data by those labels."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Pricing models and discounts: pay-as-you-go, sustained use discounts, committed use discounts and Spot VMs",
  body: [
   "Google Cloud's default pricing is pay-as-you-go: you pay for the resources you use, with no up-front commitment, and most compute is billed per second. This suits new, variable or unpredictable workloads. Google Cloud also offers ways to pay less when your usage has particular patterns, and the exam expects you to match each to a scenario.",
   "Sustained use discounts are applied automatically to certain Compute Engine resources that run for a significant portion of the billing month. The longer they run in the month, the bigger the discount. No commitment or sign-up is needed, but they apply only to some machine types, and newer machine families may not receive them.",
   "Committed use discounts (CUDs) give a lower price in exchange for committing to use a certain amount of resources, or to spend a certain amount, for one or three years. Three-year commitments give larger discounts than one-year ones. Resource-based commitments cover specific amounts of vCPU and memory in a region, while spend-based commitments apply to services such as Cloud SQL or Cloud Run. CUDs suit steady, predictable workloads that you are confident will keep running, such as production databases. The trade-off is that you pay for the commitment even if you use less.",
   "Spot VMs use Google Cloud's spare capacity at a large discount compared with standard VMs. The catch is that Google can stop (preempt) them at any time when it needs the capacity back, with only a short warning. They suit fault-tolerant, flexible work such as batch processing, rendering, testing, and some data analysis or ML training that can checkpoint and resume. They are not suitable for workloads that must run without interruption, such as a primary database or a customer-facing service without redundancy. Preemptible VMs were the earlier version of this option.",
   "Other cost levers include rightsizing with recommendations, autoscaling so capacity follows demand, choosing the right storage class, scaling serverless services to zero, and choosing lower-priced regions when location does not matter."
  ],
  terms: [["Pay-as-you-go", "Paying only for resources used, with no up-front commitment."], ["Sustained use discount", "An automatic discount for certain Compute Engine resources that run for much of a month."], ["Committed use discount (CUD)", "A lower price in exchange for a one- or three-year commitment to resources or spend."], ["Spot VM", "A heavily discounted VM using spare capacity that Google can stop at any time."]],
  example: "A media company runs its always-on production database under a three-year committed use discount, renders video overnight on Spot VMs that can restart if interrupted, and runs its variable web front end on autoscaled pay-as-you-go instances.",
  tip: "Steady and predictable for years: committed use discounts. Fault-tolerant and interruptible: Spot VMs. Automatic, no commitment: sustained use discounts. Unpredictable: pay-as-you-go.",
  check: [["Why is a Spot VM a poor choice for a primary database?", "Google can stop Spot VMs at any time, which would interrupt a service that must stay available."], ["What must a customer give in exchange for a committed use discount?", "A commitment to use a set amount of resources or spend for one or three years, paid even if usage is lower."]]
 },
 {
  t: "DevOps and Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) principles",
  body: [
   "Traditionally, developers wanted to release new features quickly, while operations teams wanted stability and resisted change. The result was slow releases, friction and blame. DevOps and Site Reliability Engineering are approaches that resolve this tension, and the exam expects you to know their main ideas.",
   "DevOps is a culture and set of practices that bring development and operations together with shared goals and responsibilities. Key practices include small, frequent releases; automation of building, testing and deployment through continuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD); infrastructure as code; monitoring and fast feedback from production; and collaboration across teams. The aim is to deliver value to users faster and more reliably at the same time.",
   "Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) is Google's way of running production systems, developed internally and shared publicly through books and practices. It is often described as a specific implementation of DevOps ideas. SRE treats operations as a software engineering problem: engineers write software and automation to run systems reliably at scale.",
   "Core SRE principles include: defining reliability targets with service level objectives (SLOs) based on what users need, rather than aiming for 100%; using error budgets to balance feature releases and reliability; reducing toil, the repetitive manual work that scales with the service, by automating it; monitoring the signals that matter to users; practicing gradual rollouts and quick rollback so changes are safer; and running blameless postmortems after incidents to learn and fix underlying causes without punishing individuals.",
   "For organizations moving to the cloud, these practices matter as much as the technology. Managed services reduce toil, CI/CD tools such as Cloud Build and Cloud Deploy automate delivery, and Google Cloud Observability provides the monitoring SRE depends on. Adopting SRE also usually means cultural change: accepting that failure will happen, learning from it openly and sharing responsibility for reliability."
  ],
  terms: [["DevOps", "A culture and practices uniting development and operations to deliver changes quickly and reliably."], ["Site Reliability Engineering (SRE)", "Google's approach to running reliable systems by applying software engineering to operations."], ["Toil", "Manual, repetitive operational work that scales with the service and can be automated."], ["Blameless postmortem", "An incident review that focuses on causes and fixes, not on blaming individuals."]],
  example: "After a failed release causes an outage, an online bank holds a blameless postmortem. It finds that manual deployment steps were skipped, automates the deployment with a CI/CD pipeline, adds automatic rollback when error rates rise, and sets an SLO for its login service.",
  tip: "SRE is Google's implementation of DevOps principles. Key words to recognize: SLOs, error budgets, toil reduction, automation and blameless postmortems.",
  check: [["What is toil in SRE, and what should be done about it?", "Repetitive manual operational work that scales with the service; SRE teams automate it to free time for engineering."], ["Why are postmortems blameless?", "So people share what really happened, allowing the organization to fix the systemic causes rather than hide problems."]]
 },
 {
  t: "SLIs, SLOs, SLAs and error budgets",
  body: [
   "Reliability needs to be measured and agreed, not just hoped for. Site Reliability Engineering uses a small set of terms that the exam tests directly. They build on one another.",
   "A service level indicator (SLI) is a carefully chosen measurement of some aspect of the service that users care about. Common SLIs are availability (the proportion of successful requests), latency (the proportion of requests faster than a threshold, such as 300 ms), and correctness or freshness of data. An SLI is a number you measure, for example: 99.95% of requests succeeded in the last 30 days.",
   "A service level objective (SLO) is the target value for an SLI over a period of time, for example: 99.9% of requests will succeed over a rolling 30 days. SLOs are internal goals chosen by the service owner, based on what users actually need. Aiming for 100% is almost never right: it is extremely expensive, slows change, and users usually cannot tell the difference beyond a certain point because their own devices and networks are less reliable.",
   "A service level agreement (SLA) is a formal contract with customers that states a level of service and the consequences, typically financial credits, if the provider fails to meet it. Google Cloud publishes SLAs for many services. SLAs are usually looser than internal SLOs, so a team notices and fixes problems before a contractual breach.",
   "The error budget is the amount of unreliability an SLO allows: 100% minus the SLO. With a 99.9% availability SLO, the error budget is 0.1% of requests, or about 43 minutes of full downtime in 30 days. The error budget turns reliability into a shared decision tool. While budget remains, the team can release new features and take reasonable risks. If the budget is used up, the team slows or freezes feature releases and focuses on reliability until the service is back within its objective. This aligns developers and operators instead of pitting speed against stability."
  ],
  terms: [["SLI", "Service level indicator: a measurement of service behavior, such as the percentage of successful requests."], ["SLO", "Service level objective: an internal target for an SLI over a period of time."], ["SLA", "Service level agreement: a contract with customers stating consequences if a service level is not met."], ["Error budget", "The allowed unreliability under an SLO, equal to 100% minus the SLO."]],
  example: "A payments API has an SLO of 99.95% successful requests over 30 days. Halfway through the month, a bad release uses 80% of the error budget. The team pauses new feature launches, adds automated rollback and resumes releases once reliability is back on track.",
  tip: "SLI is what you measure, SLO is your internal target, SLA is the external contract with penalties. Error budget = 100% minus SLO. SLAs are usually looser than SLOs.",
  check: [["A service's SLO is 99.9% availability. What is its error budget?", "0.1% of requests (or time) in the measurement period."], ["Why should an SLA usually be looser than the internal SLO?", "So the team detects and fixes problems when it misses its internal target, before breaching the customer contract."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Reliability and disaster recovery: redundancy across zones and regions, backups, RTO and RPO",
  body: [
   "Failures are inevitable: disks fail, software has bugs, people make mistakes and occasionally a whole data center or region has problems. Reliable systems are designed to keep working, or to recover quickly, when things go wrong. The cloud makes many reliability techniques easier and cheaper, but they still must be chosen and configured.",
   "High availability (HA) is about keeping a service running through failures of individual components. The main technique is redundancy: running more than one instance so that others take over when one fails. In Google Cloud that means running instances in multiple zones of a region, for example with a regional managed instance group behind a load balancer, or using regional services such as a Cloud SQL instance configured for high availability, which fails over to a standby in another zone. Health checks and autohealing replace failed instances automatically.",
   "Disaster recovery (DR) is about recovering from larger events, such as a regional outage, a major security incident or data corruption. Two metrics guide DR planning. The recovery time objective (RTO) is the maximum acceptable time a service can be unavailable before it must be restored. The recovery point objective (RPO) is the maximum acceptable amount of data loss, measured as time: an RPO of 15 minutes means you must be able to restore data to within 15 minutes of the failure. Smaller RTOs and RPOs need more expensive designs.",
   "DR patterns range from cheap to expensive. Backup and restore: regular backups stored in another region, with infrastructure rebuilt when needed; low cost but longer RTO and RPO. Warm standby: a scaled-down copy running in another region, ready to scale up. Hot or active-active: full capacity in multiple regions at once, with near-zero RTO and RPO but the highest cost.",
   "Backups themselves must be protected, tested and ideally stored in a different location and account from production. A DR plan that has never been tested is only a hope, so organizations should rehearse recovery regularly."
  ],
  terms: [["High availability", "Designing a system to keep running despite component failures, usually through redundancy."], ["Disaster recovery", "Plans and systems for restoring service after a major failure."], ["RTO", "Recovery time objective: the maximum acceptable downtime before service is restored."], ["RPO", "Recovery point objective: the maximum acceptable data loss, measured in time."]],
  example: "An online retailer runs its store across three zones in one region for high availability. For disaster recovery it replicates its database to a second region and keeps a small warm standby there, meeting its RTO of one hour and RPO of five minutes. It tests failover twice a year.",
  tip: "RPO is about data loss (how far back you restore to); RTO is about downtime (how long until you are running). Multiple zones protect against zone failure; multiple regions protect against regional disasters.",
  check: [["A business can lose at most 10 minutes of data. Which metric is this?", "The recovery point objective (RPO)."], ["Which DR pattern gives the lowest RTO and RPO, and what is its drawback?", "Active-active (hot) across multiple regions; it is the most expensive."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Google Cloud Observability: Cloud Monitoring, Cloud Logging, Cloud Trace and Error Reporting",
  body: [
   "You cannot run a reliable service without knowing how it behaves. Observability is the ability to understand the internal state of a system from the data it produces, mainly metrics, logs and traces. Google Cloud Observability (formerly known as Stackdriver and then Cloud Operations suite) is a set of integrated services for this. Much of the data is collected automatically from Google Cloud services, and agents or libraries collect more from VMs and applications.",
   "Cloud Monitoring collects metrics: numeric measurements over time, such as CPU utilization, request count, latency and error rate, from Google Cloud services, applications, other clouds and on-premises systems. You can build dashboards, create uptime checks that test whether an endpoint responds from locations around the world, and define alerting policies that notify people through email, SMS, chat tools or incident management systems when conditions are met. It also supports defining SLOs and tracking error budgets.",
   "Cloud Logging collects, stores and lets you search log entries from Google Cloud services, VMs, containers and applications, including Cloud Audit Logs. The Logs Explorer lets you filter and query logs, log-based metrics turn log patterns into metrics for alerting, and log sinks route logs to Cloud Storage, BigQuery or Pub/Sub for long-term retention or analysis.",
   "Cloud Trace is a distributed tracing system. It follows individual requests as they travel through different services, recording how long each step takes, which helps find the source of latency in microservices architectures. Error Reporting groups and counts application errors and crashes so developers see which problems are new or most frequent. Cloud Profiler analyzes the CPU and memory use of running code to find inefficient functions.",
   "Together, these tools support SRE practices: measuring SLIs, alerting on symptoms users feel, investigating incidents and improving performance. The key distinction: metrics tell you something is wrong, logs tell you what happened, and traces show where time went in a request."
  ],
  terms: [["Observability", "The ability to understand a system's internal state from its metrics, logs and traces."], ["Cloud Monitoring", "Collects metrics and provides dashboards, uptime checks and alerts."], ["Cloud Logging", "Collects, stores, searches and routes log data."], ["Cloud Trace", "Distributed tracing that shows how long each step of a request takes across services."]],
  example: "Customers report slow checkouts. A Cloud Monitoring alert confirms latency has risen, Cloud Trace shows most of the delay is in calls to the inventory service, and Cloud Logging reveals repeated timeout errors from that service's database connection. The team fixes the connection pool within an hour.",
  tip: "Metrics, dashboards and alerts: Cloud Monitoring. Searching and storing logs: Cloud Logging. Finding latency across microservices: Cloud Trace. Grouping application errors: Error Reporting.",
  check: [["Which tool would show which microservice is slowing down a request?", "Cloud Trace."], ["How can you keep logs for years for compliance at low cost?", "Route them with a log sink to Cloud Storage (for example in a colder storage class) or to BigQuery for analysis."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Google Cloud Customer Care: support plans and when to use them",
  body: [
   "Even well-run cloud environments sometimes need help from the provider: a service behaving unexpectedly, a quota that must be raised quickly, a billing question or advice on architecture. Google Cloud Customer Care offers several levels of support, and organizations choose one based on how critical their workloads are.",
   "Every customer has access to basic support at no extra cost. It covers billing and account questions and gives access to documentation, community forums and service status information. It does not include technical support cases for most problems.",
   "Paid support plans add technical support with committed response times, and higher tiers add faster responses and more proactive services. In broad terms, the lower paid tier suits small teams or workloads in development that need occasional technical help during business hours. A middle tier is aimed at production workloads and offers faster responses for high-priority cases, around the clock. The top tier, Premium Support, is for enterprises with business-critical workloads: it offers the fastest response for critical issues, a Technical Account Manager (TAM) who knows the customer's environment and acts as a trusted adviser, proactive guidance such as architecture and operational reviews, and help during planned events such as major launches. The exact names, prices and response times change over time, so check the current Customer Care page rather than memorizing numbers.",
   "When opening a case, customers set a priority that reflects business impact, from general questions to critical production outages. Accurate priority helps the right people respond. Support engineers can see only what they need, and with Access Transparency customers can see logs of any Google access to their content during a case.",
   "Beyond Customer Care, organizations can get help from Google Cloud partners, professional services, training and certification, and the large community. Choosing a support plan is part of an organization's operational and risk planning, and its cost should be weighed against the impact of downtime."
  ],
  terms: [["Customer Care", "Google Cloud's support offering, with basic and paid plans."], ["Technical Account Manager (TAM)", "A named Google adviser for Premium Support customers who knows their environment."], ["Case priority", "The urgency level set on a support case, based on business impact."], ["Proactive support", "Guidance such as architecture reviews and event planning offered before problems happen."]],
  example: "A streaming company preparing for a live global event on Google Cloud uses Premium Support. Its Technical Account Manager arranges an architecture review and event planning with Google engineers, and on the day of the event the team has the fastest escalation path if a critical issue arises.",
  tip: "Business-critical workloads that need the fastest response and a named adviser point to the top tier (Premium Support with a Technical Account Manager). Do not memorize response times; they change.",
  check: [["What does basic (free) support include?", "Billing and account support, documentation, community forums and status information, but not technical support cases for most problems."], ["Name two things the top support tier adds.", "Fastest response for critical issues, a Technical Account Manager, and proactive guidance such as architecture reviews and event support (any two)."]]
 },
 {
  t: "Sustainability: Google's carbon-free energy goals, low-carbon regions and the Carbon Footprint tool",
  body: [
   "Data centers use large amounts of electricity, so IT has a real environmental impact. Many organizations now have sustainability targets and must report their emissions, including those from cloud services. Moving to an efficient cloud and choosing low-carbon options can reduce a company's IT footprint, and the exam includes sustainability as part of operating in the cloud.",
   "Google has a long record in this area. It became carbon neutral for its operations in 2007 through a combination of efficiency, renewable energy and carbon offsets (it has since shifted its focus from offsets toward cutting emissions directly), and since 2017 it has purchased enough renewable energy each year to match its global annual electricity use. Google's goal is to run on 24/7 carbon-free energy on every grid where it operates by 2030, meaning every hour of electricity use is matched by carbon-free sources on the same grid, which is more demanding than annual matching. It also has a goal to reach net-zero emissions across its operations and value chain by 2030. Google designs its data centers for high energy efficiency, including custom hardware and machine learning used to optimize cooling.",
   "Google Cloud gives customers tools to make lower-carbon choices. The carbon-free energy percentage (CFE%) of each region is published, showing how much of the electricity used there comes from carbon-free sources on an hourly basis. In the console, regions with the lowest grid carbon intensity are marked with a low CO2 indicator, so teams can choose them for new workloads when location requirements allow. Batch jobs that are not time-sensitive can also be scheduled in lower-carbon regions.",
   "The Carbon Footprint tool, available in the console for free, reports the estimated greenhouse gas emissions associated with a customer's Google Cloud usage, broken down by project, region and product. Organizations use it for sustainability reporting and to track the effect of their choices. Data can be exported to BigQuery for analysis.",
   "Efficient practices also reduce emissions: rightsizing, autoscaling, deleting idle resources and using serverless services that scale to zero save both cost and energy."
  ],
  terms: [["24/7 carbon-free energy", "Matching every hour of electricity use with carbon-free sources on the same grid; Google's 2030 goal."], ["Carbon-free energy percentage (CFE%)", "The share of a region's hourly electricity use that comes from carbon-free sources."], ["Carbon Footprint tool", "A console tool that reports the estimated emissions of a customer's Google Cloud usage."], ["Low CO2 region", "A region marked in the console as having low grid carbon intensity."]],
  example: "A European retailer must report IT emissions each year. It uses the Carbon Footprint tool to see emissions by project, moves a nightly batch analytics job to a region marked low CO2 that also meets its data residency rules, and deletes idle development VMs, reducing both its bill and its reported emissions.",
  tip: "Know the tools: the Carbon Footprint tool reports customer emissions; low CO2 region indicators and CFE% help choose regions. Google's 2030 goal is 24/7 carbon-free energy.",
  check: [["What does the Carbon Footprint tool show?", "Estimated greenhouse gas emissions from a customer's Google Cloud usage, broken down by project, region and product."], ["How can a team reduce emissions for a batch job with no location requirement?", "Run it in a region marked low CO2 or with a high carbon-free energy percentage, and size it efficiently."]]
 }
], { reviewed: "2026-09-25" });
